.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt DQ 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dq
.Nd DescriptorQueue: file descriptors served by a user process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile the device into the kernel, place the following line in the
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "device dq"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the device as a module at boot time, place the
following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dq_load="YES"
.Ed
.Pp
The module can only be loaded during boot and never unloads.
.Pp
.In dev/dq/dq.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
device lets a process, the
.Em controller ,
mint file descriptors whose contents it serves.
A minted descriptor is an ordinary
.Vt struct file
with its own operations table and no vnode: it answers
.Xr fstat 2 ,
.Xr fstatfs 2 ,
.Xr fpathconf 2 ,
.Dv F_KINFO
and the
.Pa /dev/fd
links from an
.Em identity
the controller presented, and turns
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
the attribute calls,
.Xr fsync 2 ,
the space calls, the advisory locks and (when asked) the
.Xr ioctl 2
commands a regular file does not answer itself into
.Em events
the controller reads from its
.Em queue
and answers.
The process holding such a descriptor, the
.Em requester ,
sees a regular file.
The controller never sees the requester's memory: an event carries an
offset and a length, or the bytes the requester wrote; a reply carries
bytes.
.Pp
The interface is the installed header
.In dev/dq/dq.h ,
fixed width, native byte order, LP64 only and additive, and the
.Xr libdq 3
library wraps it.
This page describes the device as built through stage 1c-iii (served
files, directories and symlinks, attachment, served lookups, working
directory and root), and names every answer that differs from a real
file.
.Pp
A controller that attaches programs
.Pq Sx Attachment and resolution
is their
.Em Capability Broker :
the kernel puts each capability an attached program is about to obtain
to it first, at the
.Em capability lifecycle hooks ,
and it answers allow
.Pq Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS ,
deny
.Pq an errno ,
substitute
.Pq Dv DQ_RESOLVE_SWAP , Sx Descriptor substitution
or proxy
.Pq Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD , Sx Served answers .
A Capability Broker is not one of the brokered system services, the
providers that hand out capabilities on request.
Code and this page keep the word controller.
.Ss Opening the device
.Pa /dev/dq
is created mode 0600, owner root, group wheel.
Every
.Xr open 2
runs
.Fn priv_check PRIV_DRIVER
and fails with
.Er EPERM
without that privilege, with
.Er EACCES
before that when the node's mode refuses the caller; a 32-bit process is
refused with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
In stage 1 the device permission is the authority; delivery of a
rights-limited queue descriptor through the capability plane arrives with
the
.Li BSDDescriptorQueue
provider.
.Pp
One open is one queue.
Opening
.Pa /dev/fd/ Ns Ar N
of an open queue goes through
.Xr fdescfs 4 ,
which shares the existing file rather than opening the device again, so the
new descriptor is the same queue; only a path open of
.Pa /dev/dq
creates a queue.
.Ss The queue descriptor and its owner
A queue belongs to the process that opened it, its
.Em owner ,
and to no other.
Three rules follow; none can be turned off.
.Pp
.Sy The descriptor stays in the owner's descriptor table .
Whatever flags the open asked for, every descriptor of the queue
.Po
the one
.Xr open 2
returned, a
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr dup2 2
or
.Dv F_DUPFD
copy, an
.Xr fdescfs 4
reopen
.Pc
is closed in every child
.Po
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr vfork 2 ,
.Xr pdfork 2 ,
.Xr rfork 2
with
.Dv RFFDG ,
.Xr posix_spawn 3
.Pc
and at
.Xr execve 2 ,
and is refused with
.Er ENOTCAPABLE
by
.Xr sendmsg 2
with
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS ,
by
.Xr pddupfd 2
and by a send to a
.Xr mac_capability 4
service, the coalition service included.
The Linux
.Fn pidfd_getfd
answers
.Er EPERM .
.Xr cap_xfer_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_cloexec_limit 2
and
.Xr cap_clofork_limit 2
cannot loosen the descriptor
.Pq Er ENOTCAPABLE .
The close-on-exec and close-on-fork flags are set by the open and are
advisory for a queue:
.Dv F_SETFD
may clear them and a
.Xr dup 2
copy reads back 0 from
.Dv F_GETFD ,
and the descriptor is closed by fork and exec all the same.
.Dv F_GETFL
shows the open flags and nothing else; the
.Va kern.file
listing shows the kernel's mark of a pinned file, 0x80000000, in
.Va xf_flag .
.Pp
.Sy Only the owner operates the queue .
The one other process that can reach it is one that shares the owner's
descriptor table
.Po
.Xr rfork 2
without
.Dv RFFDG ,
the Linux
.Fn clone
with
.Dv CLONE_FILES
.Pc .
For that process
.Xr read 2 ,
every
.Xr ioctl 2
that reaches the queue
.Po
an unknown command included; the kernel itself answers
.Dv FIODTYPE ,
and
.Dv FIONBIO
or
.Dv FIOASYNC
that asks for the state the open file already has
.Pc
and the registration of a
.Xr kqueue 2
filter fail with
.Er EPERM ,
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLNVAL ,
and
.Xr select 2
never reports the queue ready, so a
.Xr select 2
without a timeout does not return.
.Dv F_SETFL
that changes
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
or
.Dv O_ASYNC
fails with
.Er EPERM
and leaves the flags as they were; the other status flags are the open
file's and a sharer may change them.
While such a call is being refused, a read by the owner may observe the
requested mode once.
What acts on the descriptor table and not on the queue is the sharer's as
it is the owner's:
.Xr close 2 ,
.Xr dup2 2
over the number, and
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2 ,
which then limits the owner too.
.Xr aio_read 2
is not the owner reading: it fails with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
as for any device, and with
.Er EPERM
from
.Xr aio_error 2
when
.Va vfs.aio.enable_unsafe
is set.
The owner is the process, not a credential: it stays the owner when it
changes its user or enters a jail.
.Dq Owner
means the opening process and whatever can run system calls in it; the
rule claims nothing against a process that may debug the owner.
.Pp
.Sy The queue dies with its owner .
When the owner exits, calls
.Xr execve 2 ,
or leaves a descriptor table it shares
.Po
.Xr rfork 2
with
.Dv RFFDG
or
.Dv RFCFDG
.Pc ,
the queue dies at that point, whoever still holds its file, with the
effects of
.Sx Object lifecycle .
A queue also dies when its last descriptor is closed.
A dead queue answers
.Er ENXIO
to every operation by any caller, and
.Dv POLLNVAL
to
.Xr poll 2 ;
a process sharing the dead owner's table can only close it.
The dead queue is counted in
.Va kern.dq.queues
and listed, as closing and under the pid that opened it, by the observe
service until that last close.
A Linux process whose descriptor table holds a queue, owner or not, is
refused
.Fn unshare CLONE_FILES
and
.Fn close_range
with
.Dv CLOSE_RANGE_UNSHARE
.Pq Er EINVAL ,
because the copy would drop the queue.
.Pp
A process that has opened a queue carries the flag
.Dv P2_DQOWNER
.Pq 0x40000000
in the
.Va ki_flag2
field of its
.Vt struct kinfo_proc
from then on; the flag is not inherited and is not cleared when the queue
is closed.
.Ss Events and replies
.Dv DQ_API_VERSION
is 2; version 1 had a 96-byte
.Dv DQ_OP_RESOLVE
body.
Every ioctl argument that carries a version must carry this one
.Pq Er EINVAL
and every event reports it.
.Pp
Every record on the queue starts with a
.Vt struct dq_event
of
.Dv DQ_API_VERSION ,
whose
.Va de_size
is the whole record, header and body, always a multiple of 8: a body with
a variable payload (the data of a
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE ,
the argument of a
.Dv DQ_OP_IOCTL )
is padded with zeros and its length field counts the payload alone.
Records are packed back to back in a
.Xr read 2
buffer.
.Va de_op
is the operation
.Pq Vt enum dq_op ,
.Va de_object
the object id,
.Va de_id
the reply token (0 for a record that takes no reply),
.Va de_seq
the queue's sequence number,
.Va de_caller
the requesting process (pid, parent, thread, credential, jail and
.Xr mac_capability 4
nonce;
.Dv DQ_CALLER_F_KERNEL
with pid 0 when a kernel process such as an aio daemon posted it);
.Dv DQ_CALLER_F_ATTACHED
when the requester is attached, see
.Sx Attachment and resolution, and
.Va de_time
the monotonic time of the post.
The operations:
.Bl -tag -width "DQ_OP_UNLOCK_ALL" -offset indent
.It Dv DQ_OP_READ
Body
.Vt struct dq_read_body :
.Va dr_offset
and
.Va dr_len ,
at most
.Va kern.dq.max_io .
The reply is an errno and up to
.Va dr_len
bytes; a short reply is the end of the file.
A larger
.Xr read 2
is posted as consecutive events, each waited for before the next, so the
controller sees them in order.
.It Dv DQ_OP_GETATTR
No body; posted by
.Xr fstat 2
only when the policy has
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR .
The reply is an errno and, when it is 0, exactly one
.Vt struct dq_ident_file ,
which is validated as at mint and becomes the presented identity.
.It Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
No body, no reply, posted at the object's last reference when the policy
has
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_CLOSE .
.It Dv DQ_OP_INTERRUPT
Body
.Vt struct dq_interrupt_body :
the
.Va din_id
of a delivered event whose requester gave up (a signal or
.Dv SIGKILL
while waiting).
No reply; the controller's own reply to that id is late.
.It Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
Body
.Vt struct dq_write_body :
.Va dw_offset ,
.Va dw_len
(at most
.Va kern.dq.max_io )
and
.Va dw_flags ,
followed by the
.Va dw_len
bytes.
The reply is an errno and, when it is 0, one
.Vt struct dq_write_reply :
.Va dwr_written
(at most
.Va dw_len ;
less is a short write) and
.Va dwr_offset ,
where the bytes went.
See
.Sx Writes .
.It Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR
Body
.Vt struct dq_setattr_body ;
reply an errno.
See
.Sx Attributes .
.It Dv DQ_OP_FSYNC
Body
.Vt struct dq_fsync_body :
.Va dfs_mode ,
the
.Fn fo_fsync
mode word: 0 for
.Xr fsync 2 ,
1 for
.Xr fdatasync 2 ,
2 for a whole-filesystem sync (the Linux
.Fn syncfs ) .
Reply an errno.
Posted whenever the object's policy has
.Va dp_write
.Dv DQ_EVENT ,
whatever the descriptor's own access mode (another descriptor may have
written); under
.Dv DQ_DENY
nothing can be dirty and the answer is 0 without an event.
.It Dv DQ_OP_FALLOCATE
Body
.Vt struct dq_fallocate_body :
.Va dfa_offset ,
.Va dfa_len
.Pq Xr posix_fallocate 2 .
Reply an errno; on success the presented size grows to
.Va dfa_offset No + Va dfa_len
if it was smaller and
.Dv NOTE_WRITE
(with
.Dv NOTE_EXTEND
when it grew) fires.
No
.Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE
is applied, as for a vnode.
.It Dv DQ_OP_FSPACECTL
Body
.Vt struct dq_fspacectl_body :
.Va dsc_cmd
.Pq Dv SPACECTL_DEALLOC ,
.Va dsc_flags ,
.Va dsc_offset ,
.Va dsc_len
.Pq Xr fspacectl 2 .
The reply is an errno and, when it is 0, one
.Vt struct dq_fspacectl_reply ,
the range left undone, as
.Fn vn_deallocate
reports it:
.Va dscr_offset
within the request
.Pq Va dsc_offset No to Va dsc_offset No + Va dsc_len ,
and either
.Va dscr_len
0 (nothing left: a finished request answers its end, a deallocation
that stopped at the end of file answers that offset) or
.Va dscr_offset No + Va dscr_len
equal to
.Va dsc_offset No + Va dsc_len .
The range reaches the requester unchanged.
.Dv NOTE_WRITE
fires on success.
.It Dv DQ_OP_LOCK
Body
.Vt struct dq_lock_body .
The reply is an errno, and for
.Dv DQ_LOCK_GETLK
with errno 0 one
.Vt struct dq_lock_reply .
See
.Sx Locks .
.It Dv DQ_OP_UNLOCK_ALL
Body
.Vt struct dq_unlock_all_body :
.Va dua_kind
and
.Va dua_owner .
No reply,
.Va de_id
0.
Every lock of that kind the owner holds on the object is gone.
.It Dv DQ_OP_IOCTL
Body
.Vt struct dq_ioctl_body :
.Va dio_cmd
and
.Va dio_len ,
followed by the input.
The reply is an errno and the output.
See
.Sx Ioctls on a served file .
.It Dv DQ_OP_RESOLVE
A queue-level event
.Pq Va de_object No 0
posted for a lookup by an attached process: body
.Vt struct dq_resolve_body
and five strings; the reply is
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
or an errno.
See
.Sx Attachment and resolution .
.El
.Pp
The calls of a requester and the events they post:
.Bl -column "posix_fallocate(2), fspacectl(2)" "DQ_OP_FALLOCATE, FSPACECTL" -offset indent
.It Sy Call Ta Sy Event
.It Xr read 2 , Xr pread 2 , Xr readv 2 , Xr aio_read 2 Ta Dv DQ_OP_READ
.It Xr write 2 , Xr pwrite 2 , Xr writev 2 , Xr aio_write 2 Ta Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
.It Xr ftruncate 2 , Xr fchmod 2 , Xr fchown 2 Ta Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR
.It Xr futimens 2 , Xr fchflags 2 , Pa /dev/fd/N Ta Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR
.It Xr fsync 2 , Xr fdatasync 2 , Xr aio_fsync 2 Ta Dv DQ_OP_FSYNC
.It Xr posix_fallocate 2 , Xr fspacectl 2 Ta Dv DQ_OP_FALLOCATE , FSPACECTL
.It Xr fcntl 2 locks, Xr flock 2 Ta Dv DQ_OP_LOCK
.It Xr close 2 , Xr _exit 2 Ta Dv DQ_OP_UNLOCK_ALL , CLOSE
.It Xr ioctl 2 , Dv F_SETOWN Ta Dv DQ_OP_IOCTL
.It Xr fstat 2 Ta Dv DQ_OP_GETATTR
.It a fault on a Xr mmap 2 Ns ed page Ta Dv DQ_OP_READ
.It Xr msync 2 , Xr fsync 2 , page daemon Ta Dv DQ_OP_WRITE_ASYNC
.It Xr sendfile 2 Ta Dv DQ_OP_READ_ASYNC
.El
.Pp
The page operations, posted by the pager without a waiting requester
(see
.Sx Pages ) :
.Bl -tag -width "DQ_OP_WRITE_ASYNC" -offset indent
.It Dv DQ_OP_READ_ASYNC
The
.Vt struct dq_read_body
of a
.Xr sendfile 2
filling pages, answered as
.Dv DQ_OP_READ .
.It Dv DQ_OP_WRITE_ASYNC
The
.Vt struct dq_write_body
.Pq Va dw_flags No 0, a page-aligned Va dw_offset
followed by the bytes of a run of dirty pages, answered as
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
with
.Va dwr_offset
equal to
.Va dw_offset ;
the caller is
.Dv DQ_CALLER_F_KERNEL .
.El
.Pp
A fault on a mapping posts a
.Dv DQ_OP_READ ,
like
.Xr read 2 .
.Pp
The controller answers with
.Dv DQ_IOC_REPLY
and a
.Vt struct dq_reply :
.Va dr_id ,
.Va dr_errno
(0 or an errno),
.Va dr_flags
(0 or
.Dv DQ_REPLY_F_POSTOP ,
see
.Sx Post-operation attributes ;
any other bit is
.Er EINVAL )
and
.Va dr_len
bytes at
.Va dr_data ,
copied before any lock is taken.
Replies may come from any thread in any order.
A reply fails with
.Er ESRCH
when the id is not in flight (never issued, already answered, abandoned,
revoked or expired; counted in
.Va ds_late_replies ) ,
with
.Er ECANCELED ,
for a
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_SWAP
answer only, when the kernel took and closed the answered descriptor and
the requester left before it was installed
.Pq see Sx Descriptor substitution ;
an
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
answer whose requester left fails with
.Er ESRCH ,
its descriptor still the controller's),
and with
.Er EINVAL
for a wrong version or padding, an errno outside the kernel's range, a
length above
.Va kern.dq.max_io ,
a body the operation does not take, or a reply to an operation that takes
none; a refused reply leaves the event in flight, so the controller can
answer it correctly.
The body rules:
.Bl -tag -width "DQ_OP_FSPACECTL" -offset indent
.It Dv DQ_OP_READ
at most
.Va dr_len
bytes; none with an errno.
.It Dv DQ_OP_GETATTR
exactly one valid
.Vt struct dq_ident_file
with errno 0; none with an errno.
.It Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
exactly one
.Vt struct dq_write_reply
with errno 0, with
.Va dwr_written
at most
.Va dw_len ,
.Va dwr_offset
at most
.Dv OFF_MAX
and their sum at most
.Dv OFF_MAX ,
and, unless
.Dv DQ_WRITE_F_APPEND
is set,
.Va dwr_offset
equal to
.Va dw_offset ;
none with an errno.
A
.Va dwr_written
of 0 with errno 0 for a chunk of at least one byte is accepted and
reaches the requester as
.Er EIO
(a regular file never answers a write with 0 bytes and no error).
.It Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR , FSYNC , FALLOCATE
none.
.It Dv DQ_OP_FSPACECTL
exactly one
.Vt struct dq_fspacectl_reply
with errno 0 meeting the range rule above; none with an errno.
.It Dv DQ_OP_LOCK
exactly one
.Vt struct dq_lock_reply
for a successful
.Dv DQ_LOCK_GETLK ,
whose
.Va dlr_type
is
.Dv F_UNLCK ,
or
.Dv F_RDLCK
or
.Dv F_WRLCK
with a nonnegative start and length, an end within
.Dv OFF_MAX
and a pid of at least -1; none otherwise.
.It Dv DQ_OP_IOCTL
exactly
.Fn IOCPARM_LEN dio_cmd
bytes for a successful command with
.Dv IOC_OUT
set; none otherwise.
.El
.Pp
With
.Dv DQ_REPLY_F_POSTOP
the body is followed by a post-operation block
.Pq Sx Post-operation attributes ;
the rules apply to what precedes it.
.Pp
The errno a controller may answer is allow-listed per operation.
Any other errno reaches the requester as
.Er EIO
and is counted in
.Va ds2_bad_replies ,
so that, for instance, a controller's
.Er EPIPE
can never make
.Xr write 2
raise
.Dv SIGPIPE :
.Bl -tag -width "DQ_OP_FSPACECTL" -offset indent
.It Dv DQ_OP_READ , GETATTR
any errno but
.Er EINTR .
.It Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
.Er EIO , ENOSPC , EDQUOT , EFBIG , EACCES , EPERM , EROFS ,
.Er EAGAIN , ENXIO , EINVAL , ESTALE .
.It Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR
.Er EPERM , EROFS , EINVAL , EOPNOTSUPP , EIO , EFBIG , EDQUOT , ENOSPC ,
.Er EACCES , ESTALE .
.It Dv DQ_OP_FSYNC
.Er EIO , EINVAL , ENOSPC , EDQUOT , ESTALE .
.It Dv DQ_OP_FALLOCATE
.Er ENOSPC , EFBIG , EINVAL , EIO , EOPNOTSUPP , EDQUOT .
.It Dv DQ_OP_FSPACECTL
.Er ENOSPC , EFBIG , EINVAL , EIO , EOPNOTSUPP , ENODEV .
.It Dv DQ_OP_LOCK
.Er EAGAIN , EDEADLK , ENOLCK , EINVAL , EIO .
.It Dv DQ_OP_IOCTL
.Er ENOTTY , EINVAL , EIO , EPERM , EACCES , ENXIO .
.It Dv DQ_OP_RESOLVE
.Er EPERM , ENOENT , EIO , ENXIO , E2BIG , ENOEXEC , EACCES , EBUSY ,
.Er EEXIST , EXDEV , ENODEV , ENOTDIR , EISDIR , EINVAL , ETXTBSY , EFBIG ,
.Er ENOSPC , EROFS , EMLINK , EAGAIN , ENAMETOOLONG , ENOTEMPTY , ELOOP ,
.Er EDQUOT , ESTALE , EOPNOTSUPP , EAFNOSUPPORT , EPROTONOSUPPORT ,
.Er EADDRINUSE , EADDRNOTAVAIL , ECONNREFUSED , EPROTOTYPE , EFTYPE ,
.Er EAUTH , ENOATTR , EINTEGRITY
(one list for every lookup operation);
for
.Dv DQ_ROP_SOCKET
and
.Dv DQ_ROP_SOCKETPAIR :
.Er EPERM , EACCES , EAFNOSUPPORT , EPROTONOSUPPORT , EPROTOTYPE ,
.Er EOPNOTSUPP , ENOBUFS , EMFILE , ENFILE .
.El
.Pp
.Er EINTR
is never a controller's answer, to any operation (D193): the only
.Er EINTR
a requester sees comes from the kernel's own wait, in the modes that end
on a signal
.Pq Sx Waiting, deadlines and interruption ;
a reply carrying one reaches the requester as
.Er EIO
and is counted in
.Va ds2_bad_replies .
.Ss Reading the queue
.Xr read 2
on the queue copies whole records, as many as fit, in order, and never
splits one.
A buffer smaller than a header is
.Er EINVAL ;
when the first record does not fit the call fails with
.Er ENOBUFS ;
an empty queue blocks, or answers
.Er EAGAIN
under
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
or
.Er EINTR
on a signal.
A record is taken off the queue only after its copy succeeded: a fault in
the buffer after some records were copied returns those, and a fault
before any returns
.Er EFAULT ,
with nothing lost either way.
Two threads may read one queue; each record is delivered once.
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr select 2
and
.Xr kqueue 2
.Pq Dv EVFILT_READ , with the queued bytes in Va data
report readiness.
Once the queue has died every caller is refused at entry
.Po
.Er ENXIO ,
.Dv POLLNVAL ,
see
.Sx The queue descriptor and its owner
.Pc ;
a filter registered while it lived reports
.Dv EV_EOF .
.Pp
.Va de_seq
increases by one per posted event and is assigned before the depth test, so
a dropped event leaves a visible gap.
A queue holds
.Va kern.dq.queue_depth
unread events (a controller may lower its own queue's depth with
.Dv DQ_IOC_SET_QUEUE_DEPTH ,
never raise it); when it is full an operation that waits for its answer
waits for a slot (D205), in the mode and within the deadline of its own
wait, and is counted in
.Va ds2_full_waits ;
a slot read off the queue, or withdrawn, wakes one such waiter, and a
waiter that cannot use it (its quota is full) passes it on.
A record that waits for nothing and is not exempt
.Pq Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
is dropped on a full queue and counted in
.Va ds_events_dropped .
Before that bound a requester waits for a slot when an object has
.Va kern.dq.max_inflight_per_object
unanswered events or the requester itself has
.Va kern.dq.max_inflight_per_requester .
That wait is part of the event's: the event's deadline, taken before it,
bounds the two together, and a requester that reaches it there fails with
.Er EIO ,
counted in
.Va ds_deadline_misses ,
with no event posted (the controller sees nothing of it).
.Ss Waiting, deadlines and interruption
A requester waits for its reply holding only what the operation already
held (the shared offset, the object's range lock); no vnode exists.
The policy's
.Va dp_deadline_ms
bounds the wait:
.Dv DQ_DEADLINE_DEFAULT
means
.Va kern.dq.default_deadline_ms ,
0 means no bound, and a positive value is milliseconds.
A missed deadline fails the operation with
.Er EIO
and counts in
.Va ds_deadline_misses .
.Pp
What else ends a wait depends on its mode (D192):
.Bl -tag -width "uninterruptible"
.It uninterruptible
The default for the data and attribute operations of an object
.Pq Dv DQ_OP_READ , WRITE , GETATTR , SETATTR , FSYNC , FALLOCATE , FSPACECTL , READDIR , IOCTL
and for a process's own lookups: the wait is a local file system's disk
wait, as fusefs's without
.Cm intr
is.
No signal ends it; a caught signal is handled once the operation
returns, and a stop
.Pq Dv SIGSTOP , No a debugger
takes effect then.
Only the answer, the deadline,
.Dv SIGKILL ,
the process's exit and another thread's
.Xr _exit 2
(which single-threads the process before it marks it exiting) end it,
the last three with the operation abandoned.
Another thread's
.Xr execve 2
or a core dump waits for the answer, as for a disk read in progress.
The wait sleeps in slices of 100 ms, whatever is pending, so a storm of
signals costs it nothing.
A deadline of 0 is then a hard mount: only the answer,
.Dv SIGKILL
or an exit ends the wait.
