.Ss Object lifecycle
Object ids are never reused on a queue.
An object lives until the last reference to its
.Vt struct file
is gone: descriptor table entries in every process that inherited it,
.Xr dup 2 Ns s
and
.Pa /dev/fd
reopens included.
Then its id is retired,
.Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
is posted if asked for, and nothing waits for the controller.
The reply path runs the
.Fn mac_file_notify_reply
hook without a reference on the object's file, so a requester that
closes meanwhile still makes the last close: that close waits for the
hook to return (kernel code on the controller's thread, nothing the
controller does) and then releases the
.Xr flock 2
and OFD locks before it returns, as the last close of a vnode does.
When the queue dies
.Po
its owner exits, execs or leaves a shared descriptor table, or its last
descriptor is closed, see
.Sx The queue descriptor and its owner
.Pc ,
every object
is revoked, every waiter and observer woken, and later operations on the
objects answer
.Er EIO ;
.Xr fstat 2
of an object whose policy asked for
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR
then answers from the frozen identity instead of posting.
.Ss Delivery of a minted descriptor
The minted descriptor is installed in the controller with
.Dv O_CLOEXEC ,
the policy's rights and a transfer state of
.Dv CAP_XFER_NONE :
.Xr sendmsg 2
of it over
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
fails with
.Er ENOTCAPABLE
.Pq Xr cap_xfer_limit 2 ,
while
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr fork 2
and a
.Pa /dev/fd
reopen share it.
Until the resolution stage installs served descriptors in a requester, a
requester receives one by inheritance: the controller opens its queue,
mints, forks, and the child holds the object and never the queue.
.Pp
A process whose descriptor table holds the queue is refused every operation
on the queue's objects that would post an event with
.Er EDEADLK
(the reads and writes, the attribute, sync and space calls of
.Sx Events and replies ,
the locks, the event ioctls, and
.Xr fstat 2
under
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR ) ;
it is the first check of each such operation, made before any lock or
offset is taken, so the controller is refused at once rather than left
waiting on a range one of its own requesters holds while that requester
waits on the controller; only what the generic layer refuses before the
operation is reached comes first (a read-only object's
.Xr write 2
is still
.Er EBADF ) ;
the test is by the queue's file in the caller's table, so a
.Xr dup 2
and a process sharing the controller's descriptor table
.Po
.Xr rfork 2
without
.Dv RFFDG
.Pc
are covered; a child never holds the queue.
Operations the identity answers
.Pq Xr fstat 2 , Dv F_KINFO , Xr fstatfs 2 , Xr fpathconf 2 , Xr lseek 2 , Xr poll 2 , Xr kqueue 2 , Xr close 2
are never refused.
.Pp
The
.Va f_cred
of a minted file is the controller's credential, since the mint runs in
the controller; it is visible to the
.Fa file_cred
argument of the MAC file hooks and differs from a descriptor a real
.Xr open 2
returned.
The requester's own credential is what
.Va de_caller
carries.
