.Ss Writes
A descriptor minted with
.Dv DQ_MINT_F_WRITE
turns
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr pwrite 2 ,
.Xr writev 2 ,
.Xr pwritev 2
and, where
.Xr aio 4
accepts the descriptor,
.Xr aio_write 2
into
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
events, one per
.Va kern.dq.max_io
chunk, in order, each waited for before the next and each carrying its
bytes.
.Va dw_flags
is computed as a vnode computes its I/O flags:
.Dv DQ_WRITE_F_APPEND
for an
.Dv O_APPEND
descriptor (and a
.Xr pwrite 2
on one),
.Dv DQ_WRITE_F_SYNC
for
.Dv O_SYNC ,
.Dv DQ_WRITE_F_DSYNC
for
.Dv O_DSYNC
when
.Dv O_SYNC
is not also set.
Under
.Dv DQ_WRITE_F_APPEND
.Va dw_offset
is advisory: the end of file is the controller's, and the reply's
.Va dwr_offset
says where the bytes went.
The implicit file offset of a
.Xr write 2
becomes
.Va dwr_offset No + Va dwr_written ;
a positioned write leaves it alone.
On success the presented size grows to the end of the written range if it
was smaller and
.Dv NOTE_WRITE ,
with
.Dv NOTE_EXTEND
when it grew, fires; the controller may
.Dv DQ_IOC_SETIDENT
the rest (times, blocks) at any time.
.Pp
A write takes the object's range lock as a vnode write does: the whole
file for an appending or implicit-offset write, the written range
otherwise.
A write of length 0 posts nothing and returns 0 after the checks.
A short reply ends the call with that count and posts no further chunk.
.Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE
applies as for a vnode: a write that crosses the limit is clamped to it
(the clamped tail is not posted), and one that starts at or past it fails
with
.Er EFBIG
and
.Dv SIGXFSZ ;
for an append the limit is measured at the presented size, the best the
kernel knows before the controller names its end of file.
A failure after some chunks were accepted (the controller's errno, a
deadline, a revoke, the signal of an interruptible object) ends the call
with the short count of the accepted bytes and drops the error, as ZFS
does (D194); the offset and the size already follow those bytes.
No rollback of accepted chunks is possible: a failing chunk does not undo
the earlier ones (a vnode's all-or-nothing
.Dv IO_UNIT
is a property of each filesystem, and a served file has none); a declared
difference.
.Ss Post-operation attributes
A successful reply to a
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE ,
.Dv DQ_OP_FALLOCATE
or
.Dv DQ_OP_FSPACECTL ,
or to a
.Dv DQ_OP_READ ,
may carry what the backing file says after the operation, as an NFS
server returns its post-operation attributes: with
.Dv DQ_REPLY_F_POSTOP
in
.Va dr_flags ,
the last 72 bytes of the reply are a
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct dq_postop_attr {
	uint32_t	dpa_valid;	/* DQ_PA_* */
	uint32_t	dpa_pad;	/* 0 */
	uint64_t	dpa_size;
	struct dq_timespec dpa_mtime;
	struct dq_timespec dpa_ctime;
	struct dq_timespec dpa_atime;
	uint64_t	dpa_blocks;
};
.Ed
.Pp
and the fields
.Va dpa_valid
names
.Pq Dv DQ_PA_SIZE , DQ_PA_MTIME , DQ_PA_CTIME , DQ_PA_BLOCKS , DQ_PA_ATIME
become the presented identity's.
A read's block names
.Dv DQ_PA_ATIME
only.
The flag on a failed reply, a reply shorter than the block, an
asynchronous operation, a lookup or any other operation is
.Er EINVAL
with the event left in flight.
The size is a lower bound: a block whose size is below what the
operation itself proves (the end of the bytes a write took, the end of an
allocated range) is ignored, as is one with an unknown bit, a nonzero
.Va dpa_pad ,
a size above
.Dv OFF_MAX
or a time whose nanoseconds are out of range; an ignored block is
counted in
.Va ds2_postop_ignored
and fires
.Li dq:::postop-attr
with
.Er EINVAL ,
and is never an error to the requester: the operation has happened in
the backing file, and failing it would make the program repeat it.
A larger size is applied: the backing file may have grown by another
writer.
.Pp
With no block, or an ignored one, the kernel stamps the times as a local
file system does: a write that took bytes, an allocation, a deallocation
and a change of the size set
.Va di_mtime
and
.Va di_ctime
to
.Fn vfs_timestamp ;
a read that returned bytes, from the queue or from the cached pages, sets
.Va di_atime
unless the presented file system is
.Dv MNT_NOATIME .
A controller that keeps the times itself (by
.Dv DQ_IOC_SETIDENT ,
.Dv DQ_OP_GETATTR
answers or its blocks) sets
.Dv DQ_PF_NO_STAMP
in
.Va dp_flags ,
and the kernel stamps nothing.
.Ss Attributes
.Xr fchmod 2 ,
.Xr fchown 2 ,
.Xr futimens 2
.Pq and Xr futimes 2 ,
.Xr fchflags 2 ,
.Xr ftruncate 2
and the whole-attribute path of an
.Xr fdescfs 4
.Pa /dev/fd/ Ns Ar N
post
.Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR .
Ownership governs, not the access mode: every object posts it, read-only
ones included, except
.Xr ftruncate 2 ,
which the generic layer refuses with
.Er EINVAL
on a descriptor without
.Dv FWRITE .
.Va dsa_mask
names the fields to set
.Pq Dv DQ_SETATTR_MODE , UID , GID , SIZE , FLAGS , ATIME , MTIME , BIRTHTIME ;
.Dv DQ_SETATTR_F_UTIMES_NULL
marks times set to now by a caller that needed only write permission.
An empty change
.Pq Fn fchown fd -1 -1
posts nothing.
.Pp
Before posting, the kernel applies to the presented identity and the
requester's credential the rules a vnode applies, so the controller is
asked only what it alone can decide (a read-only backing store, a stale
state):
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
mode, owner and flags need the owner or
.Dv PRIV_VFS_ADMIN
.Pq Er EPERM ;
times need the same, or write permission when they are set to now;
.It
the sticky bit on a regular file needs
.Dv PRIV_VFS_STICKYFILE
.Pq Er EFTYPE ;
a set-group-id bit outside the caller's groups needs
.Dv PRIV_VFS_SETGID ;
a set-user-id bit by a non-owner needs
.Dv PRIV_VFS_ADMIN ;
and a served identity never carries set-id bits, so either is then
.Er EPERM ;
.It
giving the file away, or to a group the caller is not in, needs
.Dv PRIV_VFS_CHOWN
.Pq Er EPERM ;
.It
flags outside the settable set are
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ;
without
.Dv PRIV_VFS_SYSFLAGS
any change of a system flag, or any change while
.Dv SF_NOUNLINK , SF_IMMUTABLE
or
.Dv SF_APPEND
is set, is
.Er EPERM ;
with it, the same under a securelevel above 0;
.Dv SF_SNAPSHOT
never toggles;
.It
an immutable or append-only identity refuses every other attribute
.Pq Er EPERM ,
and a snapshot refuses size and times, and any mode with a write or
execute bit
.Pq Er EPERM , as Fn ufs_chmod .
.El
.Pp
Two declared differences from
.Xr ufs 4
in how a whole attribute change is applied:
setting an immutable or append-only flag together with another attribute
in one change is refused with
.Er EPERM ,
where ufs sets the flags and silently skips the rest; and in the
whole-attribute path
.Pq Xr fdescfs 4
size, mode and owner are applied before flags and times (each by its own
operation, as every operations table does), so clearing an immutable flag
and changing the mode in one change fails on the immutable flag still
set.
.Pp
On success the requested fields become the identity, a modification time
earlier than the birth time pulls the birth time back with it (the body
then carries
.Dv DQ_SETATTR_BIRTHTIME ) ,
the change time is now and
.Dv NOTE_ATTRIB
fires.
.Xr ftruncate 2
holds the whole range lock and applies
.Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE
after the attribute checks, so a refused truncation raises no
.Dv SIGXFSZ .
.Ss Locks
The operations table carries
.Dv DFLAG_RECLOCK
and
.Dv DFLAG_FLOCK :
.Xr fcntl 2
.Dv F_SETLK , F_SETLKW , F_GETLK ,
the
.Dv F_OFD_*
commands and
.Xr flock 2
are
.Dv DQ_OP_LOCK
events, whatever the policy's
.Va dp_write
(a read lock is the read-only descriptor's right; a write lock on one is
the generic layer's
.Er EBADF ) .
The body names
.Va dl_op
.Pq Dv DQ_LOCK_GETLK , SETLK , SETLKW , UNLCK ,
.Va dl_kind
.Pq Dv DQ_LOCK_POSIX , FLOCK , OFD ,
.Va dl_type
.Pq Dv F_RDLCK , F_WRLCK , F_UNLCK ,
the range normalised as
.Xr lockf 9
normalises it (from the start of the file;
.Dv SEEK_END
resolved from the presented size; a negative length folded; a length of 0
to the end of the file), the owner and the pid.
A negative start is
.Er EINVAL
and an end past
.Dv OFF_MAX
.Er EOVERFLOW ,
both before any event; an
.Dv F_UNLCK
by the user is a waited
.Dv DQ_LOCK_UNLCK .
.Pp
Owners are what
.Xr lockf 9
uses: for a POSIX lock the process leader, named by its pid in both
.Va dl_owner
and
.Va dl_pid ;
for a
.Xr flock 2
or OFD lock the open file, named by a keyed hash of it (stable across
.Xr dup 2
and
.Xr fork 2 ,
distinct per open, never a kernel address) with
.Va dl_pid
-1.
A blocked
.Dv DQ_LOCK_SETLKW
waits without a deadline; a signal ends it with
.Er EINTR
and posts
.Dv DQ_OP_INTERRUPT ,
and the controller must then not grant it.
The pid a
.Dv DQ_LOCK_GETLK
reply names is the controller's to give; one that proxies through OFD
locks, as
.Xr libdq 3 Ap s
.Fn dq_serve_file
does, answers -1 where a real file names the POSIX owner (a declared
difference of that controller, not of the device).
Deadlock detection between owners is the controller's
.Pq Er EDEADLK ;
the kernel's own
.Er EDEADLK
is the rule for a process holding the queue.
.Pp
Release follows the real file.
A process holds one set of POSIX locks per file, however many opens of it
it has: on a served file the file is the identity, so a
.Dv DQ_OP_LOCK
of kind
.Dv DQ_LOCK_POSIX
on one object marks every object of the same
.Va di_dev
and
.Va di_ino
on the queue (its siblings), and closing a descriptor of any of them, or
exiting, posts
.Dv DQ_OP_UNLOCK_ALL
with
.Dv DQ_LOCK_POSIX
and the leader's pid on the object closed: the owner's POSIX locks on
the identity are gone, whichever object they were taken through.
A controller keeps POSIX owners per identity accordingly
.Po
.Xr libdq 3 Ap s
.Fn dq_serve_file
does
.Pc .
.Xr flock 2
and OFD locks are released at the last reference to the open file:
.Dv DQ_OP_UNLOCK_ALL
with that kind and the open's owner.
Both are posted without waiting, from close paths that may run without a
thread, and only when a
.Dv DQ_OP_LOCK
of that kind was ever posted on the object, so an object that never
locked costs its closes nothing.
.Ss Ioctls on a served file
.Dv FIONREAD ,
.Dv FIONBIO ,
.Dv FIOASYNC ,
.Dv FIOSEEKDATA
and
.Dv FIOSEEKHOLE
are answered locally from the identity
.Pq Er ENOTTY No for a directory ,
and
.Dv FIOBMAP2
is
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
Every other command, the
.Dv FIOSETOWN
and
.Dv FIOGETOWN
of
.Dv F_SETOWN
and
.Dv F_GETOWN
included, is
.Er ENOTTY
under
.Va dp_ioctl
.Dv DQ_DENY
and a
.Dv DQ_OP_IOCTL
event under
.Dv DQ_EVENT .
The event carries what the generic layer copied in: for an
.Dv IOC_IN
command its
.Fn IOCPARM_LEN cmd
argument bytes, for an
.Dv IOC_VOID
command of int size the integer, and nothing for an
.Dv IOC_VOID
command of size 0 or an
.Dv IOC_OUT
command alone; a raw pointer value is never shipped.
For a successful
.Dv IOC_OUT
command the reply carries exactly
.Fn IOCPARM_LEN cmd
bytes, which are copied out to the caller.
The kernel cannot tell from a command number whether its argument embeds
pointers: a controller refuses what it cannot serve with
.Er ENOTTY ,
which is also what a regular file answers for an unknown command.
.Ss kqueue notes
.Dv EVFILT_VNODE
on a served file reports what a vnode's operations raise:
.Bl -tag -width "NOTE_CLOSE_WRITE" -offset indent
.It Dv NOTE_READ
a successful read.
.It Dv NOTE_WRITE
a successful write, allocation or deallocation
.Pq Xr posix_fallocate 2 , Xr fspacectl 2 ;
with
.Dv NOTE_EXTEND
when the presented size grew.
.It Dv NOTE_ATTRIB
a successful attribute change or truncation, and
.Dv DQ_IOC_SETIDENT .
.It Dv NOTE_REVOKE
.Dv DQ_IOC_REVOKE
or the queue closing; the knote then reports
.Dv EV_EOF .
.It Dv NOTE_OPEN , NOTE_CLOSE , NOTE_CLOSE_WRITE
a mint, and a last close (by a descriptor with
.Dv FWRITE ,
.Dv NOTE_CLOSE_WRITE ) .
An object has one open file, so these can reach no knote on the object
itself; they fire on the
.Em sibling
objects, those presenting the same
.Va di_dev
and
.Va di_ino
on the same queue, which is how a vnode's other opens see them.
Objects of other identities and of other queues see nothing.
.El
.Pp
What happened elsewhere (a delete, link or rename, a write by another
client) the controller raises with
.Dv DQ_IOC_PUSH .
.Dv EVFILT_READ
reports the presented size minus the offset;
.Dv EVFILT_WRITE
is always ready.
.Ss Pages
Each object has a VM object of its own, made at the mint, whose pager
.Pq the Em dq pager
fills and cleans pages through the queue.
.Pp
.Sy Mappings .
.Xr mmap 2
of a served descriptor maps that object, as
.Fn vn_mmap
maps a vnode's.
A mapping needs
.Dv FREAD ;
a private mapping may be writable (copy on write); a shared writable one
needs
.Dv FWRITE
.Pq Dv DQ_MINT_F_WRITE
and
.Va dp_mmap_shared_write
.Dv DQ_ALLOW ,
else
.Er EACCES ,
as for a read-only descriptor on a real file.
.Dv PROT_EXEC
is refused with
.Er EACCES
when the presented statfs flags carry
.Dv MNT_NOEXEC ,
and otherwise allowed: of the policy only
.Dv MNT_NOEXEC
applies to execute permission (no execution of a served file is possible
before stage 3; declared), while
.Dv CAP_MMAP_X ,
.Dv FREAD
and the MAC mmap check still apply.
.Xr procstat 1
.Fl v ,
.Xr kinfo_getvmmap 3
and the
.Pa maps
file of
.Xr linprocfs 4
show a vnode mapping with the presented size and file id and the
presented path, against the reader as
.Dv F_KINFO
presents it
.Pq see Sx Served working directory and root :
relative to the reader's served root of the same queue, the raw
.Va di_path
for any other reader; a mapping
holds the open file, so a mapping that outlives the descriptor delays
the last close (and with it the release of
.Xr flock 2
and OFD locks,
.Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
and
.Dv NOTE_CLOSE )
to the last
.Xr munmap 2 ,
where a vnode releases at the last close (declared).
A mapping made into another process's map (not the caller's own) does
not hold the open file, so its dirty pages can be dropped when the file
is closed (declared).
.Pp
.Sy Faults .
A fault on a page that is not resident posts
.Dv DQ_OP_READ
with a page-aligned
.Va dr_offset
and a
.Va dr_len
of the faulting run, clipped at the presented size and at
.Va kern.dq.max_io ;
no page is read ahead or behind.
A short reply that reaches the presented end of the file is the end of
the file: the rest of the page is zero-filled.
A reply short of bytes the presented size says exist
.Pq offset No + length < Va di_size
is an error, not zeros:
.Dv SIGBUS
for a fault,
.Er EIO
for a
.Dv DQ_CACHE_DATA
read.
The fault waits with the object's deadline (the policy's, else
.Va kern.dq.default_deadline_ms ,
else 30 seconds: a fault never waits without bound).
A caught signal does not end the wait: the fault goes on, without catching
signals, until the reply, the deadline,
.Dv SIGKILL
or the process exiting.
.Dv SIGBUS
is raised where a vnode would raise it, for a page wholly past the end of
the file, and where the controller fails it: an error reply, a missed
deadline, a revoked object, a closed queue.
A controller must not touch its own served mappings: the fault would wait
on its own queue until the deadline and then raise
.Dv SIGBUS
(the
.Er EDEADLK
rule cannot apply to a fault; declared).
.Xr mincore 2
reports the resident pages of a served mapping.
.Pp
.Sy Write-back .
Dirty pages of a shared writable mapping are written back as
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE_ASYNC ,
a run of pages per event, by
.Xr msync 2 ,
.Xr fsync 2 ,
the page daemon, and after the last close.
Write-backs carry no waiting requester; the queue enforces the object's
deadline on each.
The bytes pending per queue are bounded by
.Va kern.dq.max_inflight_bytes ;
past it the pages stay dirty and the page daemon or
.Xr msync 2
tries again, so a stalled controller holds memory, never the system.
An accepted run is clean; a refused, short or expired one stays dirty and
is written again later.
The controller's reply returns before the pages are marked clean:
.Xr msync 2
with
.Dv MS_SYNC
and
.Xr fsync 2
wait for the completion, not the reply.
.Dv MS_SYNC
fails with
.Er EIO
when a write-back of its own range was refused or missed its deadline
(only those count; the page stays dirty, a later
.Xr msync 2
writes it), and its wait is not interruptible: it is bounded by the
deadline instead (declared).
.Xr fsync 2
first writes the dirty pages back and waits for them, then posts
.Dv DQ_OP_FSYNC .
Its
.Er EIO
counts only the write-backs it started.
.Pp
.Sy Close .
After the last reference to the open file (descriptors and mappings), the
dirty pages are written back first; when every write-back completed or
its deadline passed,
.Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
is posted, carrying
.Dv DQ_CALLER_F_KERNEL
and pid 0 (it comes from the kernel's deferred destroy, not the closing
process).
Pages whose write-back failed or expired are then dropped and counted in
.Va ds2_close_dropped .
Neither
.Xr munmap 2
nor
.Xr close 2
waits for the controller.
Until the destroy finishes the object is still listed by the
.Dq dq.observe
service.
A revoke and the queue closing drop dirty pages at once, failing every
pending page operation (data the controller had not taken is lost, as
designed).
.Pp
.Sy The data cache .
Under
.Va dp_cache
.Dv DQ_CACHE_DATA ,
.Xr read 2
is served from the object's pages, a miss posting
.Dv DQ_OP_READ
for whole pages; a second read of cached bytes posts nothing.
An accepted
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
replaces the bytes of the resident pages it covers; a refused one changes
nothing.
.Dv DQ_PUSH_INVALIDATE
drops clean pages so that they are asked for again.
.Pp
.Sy sendfile .
.Xr sendfile 2
sends from the object's pages, filling missing ones with
.Dv DQ_OP_READ_ASYNC
answered as
.Dv DQ_OP_READ ;
it runs
.Fn mac_file_check_read
and the
.Er EDEADLK
rule first.
A failed or expired
.Dv DQ_OP_READ_ASYNC
aborts the transfer and the socket reports
.Er EIO
(its pending error, as for a vnode's I/O error): the
.Xr sendfile 2
call may already have returned, or it fails with
.Er EPIPE
or
.Er ENOTCONN
from the aborted socket.
A revoke reaches
.Er EIO
through the size check of the next chunk.
An INTERRUPT the queue posts for an expired page operation is allocated
without sleeping and may be lost under memory pressure; the late reply is
.Er ESRCH
either way.
An
.Dv DQ_PUSH_INVALIDATE
that races a page-in posts that run again.
.Pp
.Sy Shrinking .
A
.Dv DQ_IOC_SETIDENT
or a served truncation that lowers the size removes the pages past the
new end and zeroes the rest of the last page.
Page operations pending past the new end are failed first: the
controller's late reply to one is
.Er ESRCH ,
a delivered one is named by
.Dv DQ_OP_INTERRUPT ,
and faults beyond the new end raise
.Dv SIGBUS .
The shrink waits for those failures, which the deadline bounds.
.Ss Attachment and resolution
A controller may
.Em attach
a process to its queue.
From then on every name the process resolves is posted to the queue as a
.Dv DQ_OP_RESOLVE
event, the naming hook, one of the capability lifecycle hooks
.Pq Sx DESCRIPTION ,
before the lookup runs, and the controller decides: it answers
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
and the lookup proceeds unchanged, or an errno and the system call fails
with it.
Stage 1c-i accepts only those two answers; served results (a minted
object, attributes, a link) come in stage 1c-iii
.Pq Sx Served answers , Sx Served directories and starts , Sx Served working directory and root .
.Pp
.Sy Attaching .
.Dv DQ_IOC_ATTACH
takes a process descriptor.
The target must be a process the caller can debug
.Pq Fn p_candebug ,
must not have exec'd since it was forked (in practice a
.Xr pdfork 2 Ns ed
child waiting to exec), must have its own descriptor table and its own
working-directory state (not shared through
.Xr rfork 2
without
.Dv RFFDG
or the Linux
.Dv CLONE_FILES
and
.Dv CLONE_FS ) ,
must hold no descriptor of the queue, and must not be attached already
(here or to another queue): otherwise
.Er EBUSY .
The caller itself is
.Er EBUSY ;
an exiting or exited target
.Er ESRCH ;
a descriptor that is not a process descriptor, a wrong version, flags or
start directories
.Er EINVAL .
The preconditions are checked and the attachment committed in one
section, so no fork, exec or descriptor transfer can slip between them.
There is no detach.
.Pp
The attach also takes the target's software authority
.Pq Xr cap_process 2
out of use, in the same section.
An authority that was valid, or installed and waiting for its image, is
marked invalid as after a prison transition, and the target's invocation
binding is dropped: the target still reports the authority's identity,
with an advanced generation, but sends on endpoints bound to the authority
fail with
.Er EPERM ,
and neither the target, its children nor an image it executes acts with
the authority again.
A target with no authority, or one already invalid, is unchanged, and so
is the caller.
Until the target executes an image the stripped authority still decides
who may debug it: a debugger that is not itself stripped gets the answer it
got before the attach; after the exec the authority does not restrict it.
The stripped target is refused as a debugger, with
.Er EPERM ,
for every process the authority still protects: its creator for as long
as the creator holds the authority, and another target stripped of the same
authority or a child of its own until that process executes an image,
including a child's
.Dv PT_TRACE_ME .
An image the target itself executes does not lift these refusals.
The target's capability discovery context and its endpoints not bound to
an authority are not affected.
ATTACH is meant for a quiesced child, one blocked waiting to be told to
exec: a lookup or a fork the target has already begun when ATTACH commits
finishes unattached (its checks are behind it), so a child that runs
freely while being attached may resolve a name or leave a descendant
unconsulted.
.Pp
.Sy Inheritance .
Every child of an attached process is attached to the same queue:
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr vfork 2 ,
.Xr rfork 2
(with or without
.Dv RFFDG
or
.Dv RFCFDG ) ,
.Xr pdfork 2
and the Linux
.Fn clone .
A process that replaces its own descriptor table or working-directory
state
.Pq Fn rfork RFCFDG , Fn linux_unshare
stays attached.
.Pp
.Sy What is posted .
One event per path-taking system call, and one per lookup the kernel makes
on the process's behalf:
.Bl -column "DQ_RCLASS_KCONSUMER" -offset indent
.It Sy Class Ta Sy Lookups
.It Dv DQ_RCLASS_USER Ta the process's own path argument: open, stat,
access, readlink, realpath, getfh, unlink, rmdir, rename and link (one
event carrying both paths), symlink, mkdir, mknod, mkfifo, the attribute
and truncate calls, execve, chdir, chroot, the extended attribute and
ACL calls, revoke,
.Dv AF_UNIX
bind, connect and datagram sendto,
.Xr socket 2
and
.Xr socketpair 2 ,
and the
.Dv INTERNAL
calls
.Pq Fn __mac_get_file , undelete , quotactl ;
.Dv DQ_RF_USER
is set
.It Dv DQ_RCLASS_KCONSUMER Ta a path the process names for the kernel to
use: ktrace, acct, swapon and swapoff, auditctl, the mount points of
mount, nmount and unmount and the
.Ql from ,
.Ql fspec
and
.Ql target
mount options, quotactl's quota file, jail paths, extattrctl and a
.Xr kldload 2
path containing a slash;
.Dv DQ_RF_KCONSUMER
is set and
.Dv DQ_RF_USER
clear
.It Dv DQ_RCLASS_KERNEL Ta a lookup the kernel originates:
the interpreter of a
.Ql #!
script and an ELF image's
.Dv PT_INTERP ,
op
.Dv DQ_ROP_EXEC_INTERP
.El
.Pp
The body names the operation
.Pq Va drs_op , Dv DQ_ROP_* ,
the class
.Pq Va drs_flags ,
the lookup flags
.Pq Va drs_rflags , Dv DQ_RF_* ,
the start
.Po
.Va drs_start_kind :
the working directory, the root for an absolute path, or a directory
descriptor
.Pc ,
the open flags or mode
.Pq Va drs_mode ,
the system call number, and five strings, each NUL-terminated after the
112-byte
.Vt struct dq_resolve_body
and the record padded to 8: the path, the second path of a rename or
link, a best-effort path of the start relative to the process's root
.Pq Dv DQ_RF_STARTPATH_VALID ,
the same hint for the second path's start
.Pq Dv DQ_RF_STARTPATH2_VALID ,
and the presented path of the process's served root
.Pq Va drs_root No and Va drs_rootpathlen ; No empty with a real root .
For a rename or link the second path's own start is described by
.Va drs_start2_kind
.Po
.Dv DQ_START_REAL_CWD ,
.Dv DQ_START_REAL_ROOT
or
.Dv DQ_START_REAL_DIRFD ,
from its own directory descriptor and leading slash; 0 when there is no
second path
.Pc
and
.Va drs_start2 :
the two paths may start from different directories.
The caller carries
.Dv DQ_CALLER_F_ATTACHED .
.Pp
An
.Dv AF_UNIX
name whose first byte is NUL (a Linux abstract name) is looked up
nowhere: its
.Dv DQ_ROP_BIND
or
.Dv DQ_ROP_CONNECT
event carries
.Dv DQ_RF_ABSTRACT ,
start
.Dv DQ_START_NONE
and the name's bytes after the NUL as the path, with the exact length in
.Va drs_pathlen
(the bytes may hold further NULs).
.Pp
.Xr socket 2
posts one
.Dv DQ_ROP_SOCKET
event and
.Xr socketpair 2
one
.Dv DQ_ROP_SOCKETPAIR
event for the pair, with no path, start
.Dv DQ_START_NONE ,
and the request in
.Va drs_domain ,
.Va drs_type
(without
.Dv SOCK_CLOEXEC ,
.Dv SOCK_CLOFORK
and
.Dv SOCK_NONBLOCK )
and
.Va drs_protocol ,
the protocol of the protocol switch the kernel found (protocol 0 for
a stream on
.Dv AF_INET
arrives as
.Dv IPPROTO_TCP ) .
For a protocol switch whose own protocol is 0 the caller's protocol
number is posted as given and not validated: for raw IP it is the IP
protocol the socket uses; for
.Dv AF_UNIX
and
.Dv PF_ROUTE
the socket ignores it and
.Dv SO_PROTOCOL
reports 0.
The event is posted after the MAC check, the protocol lookup, the
capability-mode test and the jail's address-family test, which fail with
no event, and before any descriptor is allocated: a refused socket
leaves nothing behind.
The errors keep their unattached precedence:
.Xr socket 2
with a full descriptor table fails with
.Er EMFILE
before any other test, and
.Xr socketpair 2
without room for both descriptors fails with
.Er EMFILE
after the protocol, capability-mode and jail tests; neither posts an
event.
A descriptor taken by another thread while the controller decides makes
the call fail with
.Er EMFILE
after a pass.
.Pp
Only
.Xr socket 2
and
.Xr socketpair 2
are consulted.
Sockets derived from a consulted one are not:
.Xr accept 2 ,
.Xr accept4 2
and
.Xr sctp_peeloff 2 .
Receiving a socket descriptor with
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
creates nothing and is not consulted, and children of an attached
process inherit the attachment.
Sockets the kernel creates itself, also on behalf of a request (an NFS
mount, NLM, iSCSI, the
.Xr wg 4
and
.Xr gre 4
ioctls,
.Xr ng_ksocket 4 ,
TCP and SCTP UDP tunnelling), are never consulted.
.Pp
Nothing is posted for what never reaches a lookup: refusals of
capability mode
.Pq Er ECAPMODE , Er ENOTCAPABLE ,
lookups of kernel-space paths the kernel makes for itself (module
searches, firmware, the root mount), and the file-handle calls that take
no name
.Pq Fn fhopen , fhstat No and the like .
.Fn fhlink
and
.Fn fhlinkat
name a new link and post
.Dv DQ_ROP_LINK
for it.
A lookup the kernel makes for an attached process with no intent (a
kernel-copied path no consultation covers) fails with
.Er EPERM
and is counted as unmarked.
.Pp
Some consultations are made ahead of the operation that uses the path
.Pq Xr swapon 2 , Xr swapoff 2 , No the Xr quotactl 2 Dv Q_QUOTAON No quota file :
if the path's bytes change between the consultation and the use, the
call fails with
.Er EIO
rather than use bytes the controller never saw.
An attached process's lookups never take the lockless fast path, so
every lookup is consulted and resolves exactly as it would otherwise.
The names the process passes are consulted, and from stage 1c-iii-b so
is every symbolic link a passed user-origin lookup follows
.Pq Sx Served directories and starts .
The symbolic links still followed unconsulted are those met by paths
consulted ahead or carried (the second path of
.Xr rename 2
and
.Xr link 2 ,
socket names,
.Xr swapon 8 ,
quota and
.Xr kldload 2
files, mount options), by capability-mode,
.Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH
and
.Dv RINROOT
lookups (confined to their start by the kernel), and by the kernel's own
lookups in exempt regions; a controller that must hold a name boundary
there refuses symbolic links it does not trust or creates no names it
does not control.
A user-space lookup without an intent (a kernel bug) is still consulted,
fails closed, and is counted in
.Va ds2_resolve_unmarked .
.Pp
.Sy Answering .
The reply is an errno with no body, or errno 0 with a
.Vt struct dq_resolve_reply
of a kind the event accepts
.Pq Va drs_accept ,
followed by exactly that kind's payload
.Pq Sx Served answers ;
anything else is
.Er EINVAL
to the controller and the event stays in flight.
An errno outside the operation's
.Dv DQ_OP_RESOLVE
allow-list reaches the requester as
.Er EIO .
A
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
answer to a socket event is
.Er EINVAL
to the controller (served sockets come in stage 2).
An
.Er EAGAIN
answer to
.Xr rename 2 ,
.Xr link 2
or
.Fn fhlink
reaches the requester as
.Er EIO
(those calls retry internally on
.Er EAGAIN ,
which would re-post forever).
An errno answer is the system call's result and the real namespace is
not touched: an
.Dv O_CREAT
refused creates nothing.
.Pp
.Sy Waiting .
A class 1 consultation waits with
.Va kern.dq.default_deadline_ms ;
classes 2 and 3 at most
.Va kern.dq.kernel_deadline_ms ,
never without bound.
A missed deadline fails the lookup with
.Er EIO
and posts
.Dv DQ_OP_INTERRUPT .
A lookup's wait is uninterruptible unless the queue has
.Dv DQ_QF_INTR
.Pq Sx Waiting, deadlines and interruption ;
a kernel consumer's and the kernel's own consultations never end on a
signal.
With
.Dv DQ_QF_INTR
a caught signal ends the wait: during the first consultation of a system
call's path the call restarts
.Pq Dv SA_RESTART
and posts again, the abandoned event named by an INTERRUPT; any other
wait, and every wait of
.Xr rename 2
and
.Xr link 2 ,
fails with
.Er EINTR
instead (a restarted rename could report a rename that did not happen).
A restart copies the path in again: if the bytes changed, the new bytes
are posted, never the old answer reused.
A queue at its depth makes the lookup wait for a slot, within its
deadline.
.Pp
.Sy Failing closed .
When the controller's queue dies, every waiting lookup and every later
lookup of an attached process fails with
.Er EIO ,
and every waiting and later
.Xr socket 2
and
.Xr socketpair 2
with
.Er EACCES
(which they can return):
an attached process never reaches the real namespace unconsulted.
A socket event that misses its deadline fails with
.Er EIO .
A lookup attempted while the process holds a lock the consultation could
not wait under fails closed too.
Both count in
.Va ds2_resolve_failclosed .
.Pp
.Sy Kernel paths refused .
Some kernel facilities open a file by a path they keep and use outside
any lookup the controller could see; an attached caller is refused them
with
.Er EPERM :
attaching an
.Xr md 4
vnode device, the
.Xr ctl 4
block backend's file, loading a
.Xr veriexec 8
manifest, adding an
.Xr imgact_binmisc 4
interpreter, ZFS file vdevs and the ZFS cachefile, the
.Xr netgraph 4
.Dv AF_LOCAL
ksocket bind and connect, the vnode-reclaim sysctl, the
.Xr alq 9
log path sysctls, the
.Xr oes 4
mute path, and the pNFS server paths.
A mount path option
.Pq Ql from , Ql fspec , Ql target
that is not NUL-terminated is
.Er EINVAL
for an attached caller.
.Xr unmount 2
of a path that does not resolve returns that error; it does not fall
back to matching the name against mount points.
.Pp
.Sy What an attached process cannot do
(I5): an attached process gains no privilege.
.Dv DQ_IOC_ATTACH
sets
.Dv P2_NO_NEW_PRIVS
.Po visible through
.Xr procctl 2
.Dv PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_STATUS ,
inherited, never cleared
.Pc ,
so an exec of a set-user-id or set-group-id image runs with the
unchanged credentials and installs no authority; an exec that would
change the MAC label is refused with
.Er EACCES .
.Xr setcred 2 ,
.Fn __mac_set_proc ,
the capability authority's install, and
.Xr jail_attach 2
(also through
.Xr jail_set 2
with
.Dv JAIL_ATTACH )
are refused with
.Er EPERM ,
and an attached process dumps no core.
The process can still lower its privileges.
.Ss Served answers
From stage 1c-iii-a a consultation may be answered with a served result
instead of
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS .
The kernel publishes the kinds each event takes in
.Va drs_accept ,
a mask of
.Fn DQ_RACCEPT kind ;
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
is always in it, an errno is always accepted (allow-listed), and a kind
outside it is
.Er EINVAL
to the controller with the event still in flight.
From a real start, served kinds are offered only to the first path of a
user-origin lookup (the working directory, the root or a real directory
descriptor) that is not
.Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH ,
capability-mode relative,
.Dv RINROOT ,
.Dv O_NOSYMLINKS ,
.Dv NOMAGICLINKS ,
.Dv NOXDEV
or an
.Dv O_NAMEDATTR
open; the kernel's own lookups, second paths, paths consulted ahead and
socket names take
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS ,
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
where it is allowed, or an errno only.
A lookup from a served directory
.Pq Sx Served directories and starts
never takes
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS .
.Bl -column "DQ_ROP_REALPATH, DQ_ROP_ACCESS" "FD, OK" -offset indent
.It Sy Operation Ta Sy Served kinds
.It Dv DQ_ROP_OPEN Ta FD, SWAP
.It Dv DQ_ROP_STAT , DQ_ROP_ACCESS , DQ_ROP_REALPATH Ta ATTR
.It Dv DQ_ROP_READLINK Ta LINK
.It Dv DQ_ROP_SETATTR , DQ_ROP_TRUNCATE Ta OK, FD
.It Dv DQ_ROP_MKDIR , MKNOD , MKFIFO , UNLINK , RMDIR , SYMLINK , RENAME , LINK Ta OK
.El
.Pp
The replies; each size is the whole reply
.Pq Va dr_len ,
the 8-byte
.Vt struct dq_resolve_reply
included:
.Bl -tag -width "DQ_RESOLVE_ATTR"
.It Dv DQ_RESOLVE_OK
8 bytes: the controller performed the operation (for
.Dv DQ_ROP_RENAME
and
.Dv DQ_ROP_LINK
both names are its own).
.It Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
3976 bytes in all: the reply and a
.Vt struct dq_resolve_mint
(3968):
a
.Dv DQ_KIND_FILE
or
.Dv DQ_KIND_DIR
object minted at the reply (a
.Dv DQ_KIND_SYMLINK
object adds a
.Vt struct dq_resolve_target ,
its target, whose length is its
.Va di_size ,
up to 5008 bytes in all, and answers only a lookup that does not follow
a final symbolic link), validated as
.Dv DQ_IOC_MINT
validates
.Pq Dv DQ_MINT_F_WRITE needs Va dp_write No = Dv DQ_EVENT ;
.Dv DQ_MINT_F_CREATED
only for an open with
.Dv O_CREAT ;
a
.Dv DQ_ROP_TRUNCATE
answer needs
.Dv DQ_MINT_F_WRITE ) .
The id is copied to
.Va *drm_objectp
and
.Dv DQ_REASON_NONE
to
.Va *drm_reasonp
before the object is linked; a copy that faults is
.Er EFAULT
and nothing is linked.
When the reply then fails
.Er ( ESRCH ,
the event is gone;
.Er ENXIO ,
the queue is closing;
.Er ENOSPC ,
.Va kern.dq.max_objects ;
.Er ENOMEM ,
the pager object)
the id is never used and
.Va *drm_objectp
is undefined, zeroed where possible.
Observers see both pointers as zero.
.It Dv DQ_RESOLVE_ATTR
3904 bytes in all: the reply and a
.Vt struct dq_resolve_attr
(3896):
attributes only, no object; any file type and the set-id bits are
allowed (so
.Xr stat 2
of a name can show what
.Xr fstat 2
of a served descriptor cannot: set-id bits and device numbers).
.It Dv DQ_RESOLVE_LINK
A
.Vt struct dq_resolve_attr
of an
.Dv S_IFLNK
identity whose
.Va di_size
is the target's length, then a
.Vt struct dq_resolve_target :
.Va drt_len
bytes, a NUL and zeros to the next 8-byte boundary, no inner NUL; at
most 4936 bytes.
A symbolic link's target, here and in a
.Dv DQ_KIND_SYMLINK
mint, may be empty
.Pq Va drt_len No and Va di_size No 0 ,
as
.Xr symlink 2
allows;
.Xr readlink 2
then returns 0 bytes.
.It Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
The reply and a
.Vt struct dq_resolve_target
holding an absolute, normalized real path, at most 1040 bytes: the name
is that path, and the lookup continues there from the real root
(stage 1c-iii-b,
.Sx Served directories and starts ) .
.It Dv DQ_RESOLVE_SWAP
40 bytes in all: the reply and a
.Vt struct dq_resolve_swap
(32) naming a descriptor of the controller, whose open file becomes the
result of the open
.Pq Sx Descriptor substitution .
.El
.Pp
A
.Dv DQ_OP_RESOLVE
reply may be up to
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REPLY_MAX
(8192) bytes even when
.Va kern.dq.max_io
is smaller; every other reply is bounded by
.Va kern.dq.max_io .
Controllers are native LP64 processes only.
.Pp
.Sy Completion .
The requester's system call completes from the answer in its own thread
and credential; it makes no further lookup.
An
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
open is checked as a real open of the presented file would be: the
access mode against the identity with
.Xr vaccess 9
(write access also for
.Dv O_TRUNC
with
.Dv O_RDONLY ;
.Er EROFS
for a write on a read-only file system,
.Er EPERM
for a write to an immutable file and for a write without
.Dv O_APPEND
to an append-only one; skipped when the controller said
.Dv DQ_MINT_F_CREATED ) ,
.Dv O_DIRECTORY
.Er ( ENOTDIR ) ,
a write mode without
.Dv DQ_MINT_F_WRITE
and
.Dv O_EXEC
.Er ( EACCES ) ,
and
.Dv O_VERIFY
.Er ( EAUTH
while
.Xr mac_veriexec 4
enforces: a served file has no fingerprint; ignored otherwise).
.Dv O_CREAT
and
.Dv O_EXCL
are the controller's;
.Dv O_TRUNC
is applied by the kernel after the access check, through the object's
.Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR
(so
.Dv O_RDONLY | O_TRUNC
truncates as on a real file);
.Dv O_EXLOCK
and
.Dv O_SHLOCK
post a
.Dv DQ_OP_LOCK
(a signal is
.Er EINTR ) .
The descriptor gets the policy's rights,
.Dv O_CLOEXEC
and
.Dv O_CLOFORK
only as asked, and cannot be passed in
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
.Er ( ENOTCAPABLE ,
until stage 1c-iv).
An
.Dv O_PATH
open of a served file answers as an
.Dv O_PATH
vnode descriptor does: no
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
truncation,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr fchmod 2 ,
.Xr fchown 2 ,
.Xr sendfile 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2
or
.Xr getdirentries 2
.Er ( EBADF ) ,
.Dv POLLNVAL ,
kevents only with
.Dv FKQALLOWED ,
no locks but the POSIX release of a close;
.Xr fstat 2 ,
.Xr fstatfs 2 ,
.Xr fpathconf 2 ,
.Dv F_KINFO ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr fsync 2
and attribute changes through
.Xr futimens 2
as on the object.
.Pp
Vnode MAC checks do not apply to served results (there is no vnode);
served files are checked at file level as minted ones are.
.Pp
.Dv DQ_ROP_SETATTR
and
.Dv DQ_ROP_TRUNCATE
events carry a
.Vt struct dq_setattr_body
after the strings at the next 8-byte boundary: the requested change, with
.Dv DQ_SETATTR_ATIME_NOW
and
.Dv DQ_SETATTR_MTIME_NOW
for
.Dv UTIME_NOW
(the kernel's time filled in), a time left out for
.Dv UTIME_OMIT ,
and in
.Va dsa_priv
the
.Dv DQ_SAPRIV_*
privileges the kernel evaluated, only where the change can need one
(chmod: admin, and setgid when setting
.Dv S_ISGID ;
chown to another user or a group the caller is not in: chown and admin;
chflags: admin, and system flags and the securelevel when setting
system flags; utimes with explicit times: admin).
A bit not evaluated is 0.
A chflags word wider than 32 bits posts no flags and accepts no
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_OK .
Answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_OK ,
the controller applied the change; answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD ,
the kernel applies it through the transient descriptor with the rules
of
.Xr fchmod 2 ,
.Xr fchown 2 ,
.Xr futimens 2
and
.Xr fchflags 2
on a served file (the object's
.Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR
follows), and for a truncation
.Xr truncate 2 Ns 's
write access on the identity,
.Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE
and the object's
.Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR .
A chmod adding set-id bits, and a truncation past
.Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE ,
do not accept
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_OK :
a served identity never carries set-id bits (so
.Ql chmod g+s
of a served name fails with
.Er EPERM ) ,
and the signal and
.Er EFBIG
come from the kernel.
.Dv DQ_ROP_SYMLINK
carries the target as the second path (data; no start);
.Dv DQ_ROP_MKNOD
the device in
.Va drs_dev ;
.Dv DQ_ROP_OPEN ,
.Dv DQ_ROP_MKDIR ,
.Dv DQ_ROP_MKNOD ,
.Dv DQ_ROP_MKFIFO
and
.Dv DQ_ROP_SYMLINK
the creation mode after the umask in
.Va drs_cmode ;
.Dv DQ_ROP_RENAME
its flags in
.Va drs_mode .
.Pp
.Sy After the reply .
An answer the requester cannot use (a check above failing,
.Er EMFILE
at install,
.Er ENFILE
when the file cannot be allocated, an object revoked or its queue closed
before the requester bound it,
.Er EIO )
retires the object: every id whose reply returned 0 ends exactly once,
with
.Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
when the policy subscribes, and is counted in
.Va ds2_resolve_retired
when no descriptor was ever installed.
Served answers are counted in
.Va ds2_resolve_served .
.Pp
.Sy Empty paths .
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
on a served descriptor, attached or not, is the descriptor's own
operation with no event:
.Xr fstatat 2 ,
.Xr fchmodat 2 ,
.Xr fchownat 2 ,
.Xr utimensat 2 ,
.Xr chflagsat 2
and
.Xr faccessat 2
answer as their descriptor forms;
.Xr readlinkat 2
returns a served symbolic link's target and is
.Er EINVAL
for any other served descriptor;
.Xr linkat 2
is
.Er EXDEV .
.Xr fchmodat 2
and
.Xr fchownat 2
apply the change to the object itself, so they succeed on a served
descriptor opened
.Dv O_PATH
as on a real one (stage 1c-iii-b), while
.Xr fchmod 2
and
.Xr fchown 2
of that descriptor stay
.Er EBADF .
An empty path on a real descriptor is consulted but accepts only
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
or an errno: the descriptor's real operation applies.
.Dv O_EMPTY_PATH
on a served descriptor is the exception: an attached process's
.Xr openat 2
of a descriptor of its own queue posts
.Dv DQ_ROP_OPEN
with
.Dv DQ_RF_EMPTYPATH
from that object
.Pq Dv DQ_START_SERVED
and takes
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
or an errno: the controller reopens it.
.Ss Descriptor substitution
Descriptor substitution is the substitute answer of a Capability Broker:
it answers an open with a real open file of the
controller's: the
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_SWAP
answer, called a swap below.
An open
.Pq Dv DQ_ROP_OPEN
that offers
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
also offers
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_SWAP :
the controller answers with a descriptor it holds, and the kernel installs
that open file in the requester as the result of its
.Xr open 2 .
A lookup from a served start that carries a restriction in
.Va drs_rflags
.Po
.Dv DQ_RF_STRICTREL ,
.Dv DQ_RF_BENEATH ,
.Dv DQ_RF_RINROOT ,
.Dv DQ_RF_NOXDEV ,
.Dv DQ_RF_NOSYMLINKS
or
.Dv DQ_RF_NOMAGICLINKS :
capability mode, a start descriptor with limited rights,
.Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH
and the like
.Pc
offers
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
and not
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_SWAP :
the kernel checks a served answer against the start's presented path, and
has no presented path to check a real file against.
Under a served root a swapped file is not checked against the root, and it
reports its real path.
.Pp
The requester then holds the real open file.
Nothing it does with the descriptor afterwards posts an event, the
controller cannot revoke it, and it keeps working when the queue dies.
Where one of those matters, answer
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct dq_resolve_swap {
	int32_t		drw_fd;		/* a descriptor of the controller */
	uint32_t	drw_flags;	/* DQ_SWAP_F_* */
	uint64_t	drw_spare[3];	/* 0 */
};
.Ed
.Pp
The reply takes the file out of the controller's descriptor table by
number while it validates, and once the file is admitted closes the
controller's descriptor: after a reply that returned 0 or
.Er ECANCELED
the number is no longer the controller's, and the controller must not
close it (the number may already name another file).
The reply's three outcomes:
.Bl -tag -width "ECANCELED"
.It 0
The requester received the file.
.It Bq Er ECANCELED
The file was admitted and the descriptor closed, but the requester left
before the installation; the file was dropped.
Only a swap answers it.
.It any other error
The descriptor is still the controller's, as listed below.
.El
The kernel resolves no name for a swap and creates nothing: which object
the requester gets, and whether it exists, is the controller's own
.Xr open 2 .
.Pp
The supplied open file must present as what the requester's open would
have produced, and carry no state of its own that the requester's open
would not have (see
.Er EFTYPE
below).
An
.Dv O_CREAT | O_EXCL
request with
.Dv O_SHLOCK
or
.Dv O_EXLOCK
is the exception: the controller takes the
.Xr flock 2
lock on the file it creates before it answers, so that no other opener
can come between the creation and the lock; the completion then takes the
requester's lock without waiting, and a lock it cannot take at once (an
upgrade a third opener blocks) ends the open with
.Er EIO ,
counted in
.Va ds2_bad_replies .
A refusal is the reply's error; the descriptor stays open and, except for
.Er ESRCH ,
the event stays in flight and nothing is consumed (a
.Dv CAP_XFER_ONCE
entry stays usable), so the controller may answer again:
.Bl -tag -width "ENOTCAPABLE"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Va drw_fd
is not open.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
The file cannot be passed between processes; or it is not a vnode (a
served descriptor, a socket, a pipe, a shared memory object); or the
request is a full open and the vnode is neither a regular file nor a
directory.
Devices and FIFOs are refused because their open acts in the opener's
context, which a swap does not run for the requester.
An
.Dv O_PATH
request takes an
.Dv O_PATH
file of any vnode.
.It Bq Er ENOTCAPABLE
The controller's entry may not be transferred
.Pq Xr cap_xfer_limit 2 ;
or the request has
.Dv O_SHLOCK
or
.Dv O_EXLOCK
and the entry lacks
.Dv CAP_FLOCK .
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The open file has a reference besides the entry: it was duplicated,
inherited by or passed to another process, is in use by another thread of
the controller, or was already swapped and not closed.
The last two can be transient.
.It Bq Er EFTYPE
The request's path ends in a slash and the file is not a directory; the
file's offset is not 0; the file holds an OFD lock; the file holds a
.Xr flock 2
lock and the request has neither
.Dv O_SHLOCK
nor
.Dv O_EXLOCK ;
the request is an
.Dv O_CREAT | O_EXCL
open with
.Dv O_SHLOCK
or
.Dv O_EXLOCK
and the file holds no
.Xr flock 2
lock; the controller's descriptor has a locked close-on-exec or
close-on-fork state
.Pq Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH
set on it, or mmap or lookups restricted to capability mode
.Pq Xr cap_mmap_capmode 2 ;
or the file's access mode
.Pq read, write, execute or search, Dv O_PATH
or one of the status flags
.Dv O_APPEND , O_NONBLOCK , O_SYNC , O_DSYNC , O_DIRECT , O_ASYNC
differs from the request's
.Pq Va drs_mode ;
or the request has
.Dv O_DIRECTORY
and the file is not a directory; or the file is a directory and the
request opens for writing or truncation, or has
.Dv O_CREAT
without
.Dv O_DIRECTORY ;
or the file is a symbolic link and the request is not an
.Dv O_PATH | O_NOFOLLOW
open without
.Dv O_EXEC .
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The reply is not 40 bytes, a spare word or an unknown flag is set, or
.Dv DQ_SWAP_F_CREATED
answers a request without
.Dv O_CREAT .
.It Bq Er ESRCH
The requester is no longer waiting, and the file was not taken.
.El
.Pp
Flags:
.Bl -tag -width "DQ_SWAP_F_CREATED"
.It Dv DQ_SWAP_F_NOXFER
The requester's descriptor is installed
.Dv CAP_XFER_NONE :
it cannot be sent over a socket or duplicated into another process.
This narrows what the descriptor allows; it does not confine the object,
which the requester can still reopen by name where a lookup is passed.
Without the flag the descriptor carries the transfer state of the
controller's entry.
.It Dv DQ_SWAP_F_CREATED
The file did not exist and the controller created it for this
.Dv O_CREAT
request.
.Xr open 2
does not check access to a file it has just created
.Pq a file created with mode 0444 is returned open for writing ,
and the completion does the same, and does not truncate, when the file is
a regular file owned by the requester's effective user with no immutable
or append-only flag.
Otherwise the flag is ignored and the file is checked as an existing one.
.El
.Pp
.Sy Completion .
The requester's thread completes the open under its own credential, in
the order of
.Xr open 2 :
the MAC check for receiving a descriptor; the MAC open check and then
.Xr VOP_ACCESS 9
on the vnode with the access the request's flags ask for, so an open the
requester could not have made of that object itself fails with the errno
its own open would have returned; the
.Xr flock 2
of
.Dv O_SHLOCK
or
.Dv O_EXLOCK
(interruptible;
.Er EINTR ,
never a restart); the truncation of
.Dv O_TRUNC ;
then the lowest free descriptor number, with the rights of the
controller's entry limited by its transfer ceiling and, from a served
start descriptor, by that descriptor's, and with
.Dv O_CLOEXEC
and
.Dv O_CLOFORK
as the request asked.
.Xr VOP_OPEN 9
is not run again: the file had its one open, in the controller.
Two errnos are the completion's own.
The open fails with
.Er EBADF
when the supplied vnode was reclaimed after the reply
.Pq a forced unmount .
An
.Dv O_PATH
file the controller could read is usable with
.Xr kqueue 2 ;
when the requester may not read the node the open fails with
.Er EACCES ,
where its own
.Dv O_PATH
open would have returned a descriptor without that use.
A failure in the completion is the requester's errno;
the file is dropped and the controller is not told.
.Pp
The last two steps are the queue's.
A descriptor is installed only while the queue lives: when the queue has
died between the reply and the installation
.Po
its owner exited, called
.Xr execve 2
or left a shared descriptor table, or its last descriptor was closed, see
.Sx The queue descriptor and its owner
.Pc ,
the open fails with
.Er EIO ,
the errno of every lookup of an attached process from then on, and nothing
is installed.
The test is made as the completion starts, before the lock and the
truncation, and again as the descriptor is entered in the table, where it
is atomic with the queue's death; a queue that dies between the two leaves
an open that failed with
.Er EIO
after its
.Dv O_TRUNC
truncated the file, as an
.Xr open 2
that fails with
.Er EMFILE
has truncated.
An open that completed before the queue died keeps its descriptor.
.Er EMFILE
comes before this last test.
The file keeps the credential of the controller's open
.Pq Va f_cred
and the rights of the controller's descriptor; see
.Sx The substituted file and the restricted Capability Broker .
.Pp
.Sy Duties of a swapping controller .
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Restrict yourself before you answer: open the queue, attach the program,
then give up privilege
.Pq and enter a jail or capability mode where that fits ,
and only then open the files you hand over
.Pq Sx The substituted file and the restricted Capability Broker .
Hold no descriptor from before that you will hand over.
.It
Open the file afresh for each answer, with the request's access mode and
status flags and with
.Dv O_CLOFORK ,
and do not close the descriptor after a reply that returned 0 or
.Er ECANCELED :
the kernel has closed it.
The requester is then the only holder: the offset, the status flags and
any
.Xr flock 2
are its own.
.It
Do not open with
.Dv O_TRUNC :
the kernel truncates for the requester, after checking the requester's
permission to write.
.It
Hand over only objects the requester's credential may open; create files
under the requester's user, or give them to it, and declare them with
.Dv DQ_SWAP_F_CREATED .
.It
.Dv O_EXCL
is the controller's to honour: answer
.Er EEXIST
when the name exists.
The kernel resolves no name for a swap and cannot know.
.It
For an
.Dv O_PATH
answer to a requester that may not read the node, open the file under the
requester's credential, so that it carries no
.Xr kqueue 2
permission.
.It
Decide by the identity of the object opened
.Pq Xr fstat 2 ,
not by its name:
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
and
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
make the kernel resolve a name again after the decision, which is only
sound where the attached processes cannot change the directories on the
way.
.It
The path of the supplied file is visible to the requester
.Pq Dv F_KINFO , Xr procstat 1 ;
answer an open of that path as any other.
.It
Attached processes are not killed when the queue dies: a requester that
outlives its controller keeps every swapped descriptor, and is given no
new one.
.El
.Ss The substituted file and the restricted Capability Broker
A swap hands over the controller's open file exactly as it is.
The requester's descriptor is the same open file, with the credential the
controller's thread had when it opened the file
.Pq Va f_cred
and with the rights, the
.Xr fcntl 2
limit and the
.Xr ioctl 2
list of the controller's descriptor, limited by its transfer ceiling.
This is also so when the controller is another user than the requester.
It is what passing a descriptor over a socket does, and the kernel compares
no credential.
.Pp
What the requester may open is still its own: the completion checks the
requester's MAC and file permission on the object
.Pq Sx Descriptor substitution ,
unless
.Dv DQ_SWAP_F_CREATED
is honoured.
What the file's credential decides afterwards is how the file system
treats writes through the descriptor:
.Bl -tag -width "tmpfs" -compact
.It ufs
Whether a write clears the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits; whether
blocks are allocated from the reserved space
.Pq minfree ;
whether the quota limits are enforced.
.It tmpfs
Whether a write clears the set-ID bits.
.It zfs
Whether a write clears the set-ID bits.
.El
.Pp
The same credential is used for the truncation of
.Dv O_TRUNC
and for a later
.Xr ftruncate 2 .
Quota is charged to the owner of the file whatever the credential; on zfs
the quota limits do not depend on the credential.
Each
.Xr read 2
and
.Xr write 2
is checked by the MAC policies with the calling process's credential, and
.Xr fchmod 2 ,
.Xr fchown 2 ,
.Xr fchflags 2
and
.Xr futimens 2
use the calling process's credential, as for any descriptor.
Other file systems
.Pq nfs , fusefs
are not described here.
.Pp
A file opened by root therefore gives the requester root's treatment: its
writes keep set-ID bits, may fill the reserved blocks and are not held to
a quota.
The kernel does not prevent this.
.Sy The bound on what a controller can hand over is what the controller itself can still open :
.Bl -tag -width "capability mode"
.It unprivileged user
A controller that has given up root
.Pq Xr setuid 2
hands over files opened without privilege: all three treatments above are
the ordinary ones, also when its user is not the requester's.
This is the only step that gives the requester the ordinary treatment
on all three counts.
.It jail
A controller that has entered a jail
.Pq Xr jail_attach 2
can open, and so hand over, only what is under the jail's root.
Root in a jail still keeps set-ID bits and may use the reserve; only the
quota exemption is gone.
.It capability mode
A controller in capability mode
.Pq Xr cap_enter 2
can open only beneath the directory descriptors it holds.
The rights of such a directory descriptor are the rights of every file
opened beneath it, and so of every descriptor handed over; include what
the requesters need
.Po
for example
.Dv CAP_READ , CAP_WRITE , CAP_SEEK , CAP_FSTAT , CAP_FCNTL , CAP_FLOCK ,
.Dv CAP_MMAP_RW
.Pc ,
and
.Dv CAP_LOOKUP ,
which the controller's own
.Xr openat 2
beneath the directory needs, and
.Dv CAP_CREATE
when the controller creates the files it hands over.
Capability mode does not change the credential.
.El
.Pp
The queue is opened first, since that needs privilege, and keeps working
afterwards: no operation on the queue descriptor checks privilege, the
jail or capability mode again, except that
.Dv DQ_IOC_ATTACH
checks the right to debug its target with the controller's present
credential (step 2 below), and the queue belongs to the process that
opened it, not to a credential.
The same process must do all of it; the queue does not survive
.Xr execve 2 .
The order is:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Open the queue, and anything else that needs privilege or a path outside
the restrictions to come.
.It
Create the program, let it enter its own jail and change its own user,
and attach it while it waits.
.Dv DQ_IOC_ATTACH
needs the right to debug the target.
A controller that has given up root cannot attach anything it creates: a
child forked after the change of user inherits its set-ID mark and is
refused
.Pq Er EPERM ,
and one that has called
.Xr execve 2
is refused
.Pq Er EBUSY .
A jailed controller cannot attach a process outside its jail
.Pq Er ESRCH .
A program created after the controller entered capability mode is itself
in capability mode and is never offered a swap.
.It
Enter the jail.
.Xr jail_attach 2
fails with
.Er EPERM
while any directory descriptor is open.
.It
Give up privilege:
.Xr setgroups 2 ,
.Xr setgid 2 ,
.Xr setuid 2 .
.It
Open the directory descriptors to serve from, and limit them.
.It
Enter capability mode.
.It
Let the program run, and open each file handed over only now.
.El
.Pp
A descriptor the controller opened before it restricted itself keeps the
credential and the reach of that earlier open and can still be handed
over; hold none that you will answer with.
Between jails, a directory handed to a requester in another jail with
another root is installed
.Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH ,
by the jail of the credential that opened it; a regular file is handed
over across jails without a jail test, and a requester on the host gets
no such limit.
.Pp
The controller may run as the requester's user or as a user of its own;
this page recommends neither.
A controller that takes the requester's own user can be signalled by the
requester.
A requester that kills its controller keeps what it holds and obtains
nothing new.
A controller under a different unprivileged user avoids this; a file it
creates is then not the requester's, and
.Dv DQ_SWAP_F_CREATED
is not honoured for it.
.Pp
The credential the queue reports for its controller
.Pq to observers and to MAC policies
is the one that opened the queue and does not change when the controller
gives up privilege.
.Ss Served directories and starts
Stage 1c-iii-b serves directories and symbolic links and lets a served
directory start lookups.
.Pp
.Sy Kinds .
A
.Dv DQ_KIND_DIR
object presents an
.Dv S_IFDIR
identity under a policy that never writes, executes or maps shared
.Pq Va dp_write , dp_exec , dp_mmap_shared_write No = Dv DQ_DENY ,
.Va dp_cache
.Dv DQ_CACHE_NONE
or
.Dv DQ_CACHE_ATTR ,
no
.Dv DQ_MINT_F_WRITE ) ;
.Dv DQ_IOC_MINT
mints one as it mints a file.
A
.Dv DQ_KIND_SYMLINK
object presents an
.Dv S_IFLNK
identity whose
.Va di_size
is its target's length (1 to
.Dv DQ_PATH_MAX
\- 1, kept by
.Dv DQ_IOC_SETIDENT
and a
.Dv DQ_OP_GETATTR
reply), under the same policy with
.Va dp_ioctl
.Dv DQ_DENY ;
it exists only as an answer to an
.Dv O_PATH | O_NOFOLLOW
open (any other open of it is
.Er EMLINK ,
as on a real symbolic link) or to an operation that does not follow it
.Pq Xr lchmod 2 , Xr lchown 2 , Xr lutimes 2 .
Its descriptor is always
.Dv O_PATH ;
.Xr readlinkat 2
with an empty path returns the target.
A mode, owner or time change answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
with a symbolic link is applied to the object directly with the rules of
.Xr fchmod 2
and
.Xr fchown 2
(the object's
.Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR
follows).
.Pp
An open answered with a directory is checked as a real open of a
directory: a write mode,
.Dv O_TRUNC ,
and
.Dv O_CREAT
without
.Dv O_PATH
are
.Er EISDIR ;
.Dv O_SEARCH
.Pq Dv O_EXEC
needs search permission on the identity.
On a directory descriptor
.Xr read 2
is
.Er EISDIR ,
.Xr mmap 2
and
.Xr sendfile 2
are
.Er EINVAL
.Po
.Xr mmap 2
after its protection checks, as
.Fn vn_mmap
makes them:
.Er EACCES
for a shared writable mapping of a read-only descriptor comes first
.Pc ,
.Dv SEEK_DATA
and
.Dv SEEK_HOLE
are
.Er EINVAL ,
.Dv FIOSEEKDATA ,
.Dv FIOSEEKHOLE
and
.Xr fcntl 2
.Dv F_READAHEAD
are
.Er ENOTTY
(the generic layer's answers; a file system answering
.Dv FIOSEEKDATA
itself may differ, ufs answers
.Er EINVAL ) ,
.Xr posix_fadvise 2
is
.Er ENODEV ,
and
.Dv F_KINFO
and
.Xr procstat 1
report the type by kind
.Pq Dv KF_VTYPE_VDIR , KF_VTYPE_VLNK .
.Pp
.Sy READDIR .
.Xr getdirentries 2
(and so
.Xr readdir 3 )
of a served directory posts
.Dv DQ_OP_READDIR
with a
.Vt struct dq_readdir_body
(16 bytes):
.Va drd_cookie ,
the descriptor's offset, which holds the controller's cookie (0 at open;
.Xr lseek 2
sets it), and
.Va drd_len ,
the space the caller left, at most
.Va kern.dq.max_io
\- 16.
A buffer smaller than a
.Vt struct dirent
is
.Er EINVAL
with no event.
The reply is an errno
.Pq Er EIO , ENOENT , EACCES , EINVAL , ENOTDIR , ESTALE , EINTEGRITY ;
anything else is
.Er EIO
and counted) or a
.Vt struct dq_readdir_reply
(16 bytes:
.Va drdr_next ,
the cookie after the records, at most
.Dv OFF_MAX ;
.Va drdr_eof ,
0 or 1;
.Va drdr_len )
followed by
.Va drdr_len
bytes of
.Vt struct dirent
records as
.Xr getdirentries 2
returns them.
Every record is checked:
.Va d_reclen
a multiple of 8, at least
.Fn _GENERIC_DIRLEN d_namlen
and within the reply;
.Va d_namlen
1 to
.Dv MAXNAMLEN ;
the name NUL-terminated with no
.Ql /
and no inner NUL;
.Va d_type
a
.Dv DT_*
value, and
.Dv DT_DIR
or
.Dv DT_UNKNOWN
for
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql .. ;
.Va drdr_len
at most
.Va drd_len .
Zero bytes with
.Va drdr_eof
0 to a request that could hold an entry is refused.
A malformed reply is
.Er EINVAL
to the controller and the event stays in flight.
The kernel zeroes the pads, sets the offset to
.Va drdr_next ,
and reports the starting cookie as the base.
.Pp
.Sy Starts .
A served directory used as the directory of an
.Fn *at
call, by a process attached to its queue, is the lookup's start: the
lookup is posted with
.Dv DQ_START_SERVED ,
.Va drs_start
the directory's object, and its presented path as the start hint, and it
never takes
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
(that would mean a real lookup of a name that is not there); it takes
the operation's served kinds,
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
where allowed, or an errno.
An unattached process (the controller included) gets
.Er EACCES
and a process attached to another queue
.Er EDEADLK ,
with no event; a revoked directory, or one whose queue is closing,
.Er EIO .
A served file or symbolic link as a directory is
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as before.
An absolute path ignores the descriptor, as it always does.
The kernel's own lookups, kernel consumers and unmarked lookups never
start at a served directory
.Pq Er ENOTDIR .
An
.Dv O_NAMEDATTR
open from one is
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Pp
Restrictions apply as from a real directory descriptor: its rights,
.Dv FD_RESOLVE_BENEATH ,
capability mode and
.Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH .
Under
.Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH
or capability mode, before posting, an absolute path, a
.Dv RINROOT
lookup, a
.Ql ..
as the first component (after empty and
.Ql \&.
ones, so
.Ql ./../x
too), and in capability mode or from a descriptor with limited rights any
.Ql ..
while
.Va vfs.lookup_cap_dotdot
is 0, are
.Er ENOTCAPABLE
with no event.
Every other path is posted as given (under
.Dv RINROOT
too, unrewritten; the controller clamps): only the controller knows
its symbolic links.
After the reply, a served object, attributes or a link answered to a
restricted lookup must be under the start's presented path at a
component boundary
.Pf ( Pa /a
does not contain
.Pa /ab ) ,
else
.Er ENOTCAPABLE ;
under
.Dv NOXDEV
(Linux
.Fn openat2 )
on the start's file system
.Pq Va ds_fsid , di_dev ,
else
.Er EXDEV ;
the object retires.
This check sees only the result: a walk that leaves the start's file
system and comes back is the controller's to refuse.
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_OK
carries no identity and is not checked.
A served descriptor opened from a directory gets that directory's
rights, fcntl and ioctl limits intersected with the policy's (as a real
.Xr openat 2
from a limited descriptor gets the descriptor's) and inherits its
.Dv FD_RESOLVE_BENEATH .
The second path of a two-path operation is checked against its own
descriptor before anything is posted: an empty second path is
.Er ENOENT ,
another queue's served directory
.Er EXDEV ,
and a served second start must pass the operation's rights on it and
the pre-checks above for its own restrictions (posted as
.Dv DQ_RF_STRICTREL2
and
.Dv DQ_RF_BENEATH2 ) .
A real second start that is restricted or that its lookup would refuse
(a bad or limited descriptor, a file, capability mode, an empty path)
takes only
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS ,
so its own lookup runs and reports in the usual order; behind a served
first path those errors
.Pq Er ENOENT , ECAPMODE , ENOTCAPABLE No for missing rights or a Xr cap_lookup_capmode 2 No descriptor, Er ENOTDIR
are returned before posting, ahead of the mixed-start
.Er EXDEV
.Pq Fn rename \(dqmissing\(dq badfd \(dqx\(dq No is Er ENOENT .
A path consulted ahead under capability mode or from a limited or
.Dv FD_RESOLVE_BENEATH
descriptor takes no
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL .
A
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
rewrite of a path ending in
.Ql / ,
.Ql \&.
or
.Ql ..
ends in
.Ql /
(so
.Ql file/.
stays
.Er ENOTDIR ) .
.Pp
Two-path operations
.Pq Xr rename 2 , Xr link 2
whose paths both start at served directories of the same queue are one
event
.Pq Va drs_start , drs_start2 ,
answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_OK
or an errno; a served and a real start (or another queue's) are
.Er EXDEV
with no event, as is
.Xr fhlink 2
into a served directory.
.Xr funlinkat 2
with a served descriptor names its object in
.Va drs_object
and needs a path from a served start
.Pq Er EDEADLK No otherwise, with no event ;
a real descriptor with a served path is
.Er EDEADLK .
.Xr linkat 2
of a served descriptor with
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
is
.Er EXDEV .
A name under
.Pa /dev/fd/N
of a served descriptor stays
.Er ENOTDIR .
.Pp
.Sy REAL .
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
answers a name with an absolute, normalized real path: the kernel
replaces the path with it and continues from the process's real root
(never the Linux ABI root, whose retry does not run after it).
It is accepted for user-origin lookups of any operation except
.Xr rename 2
and
.Xr link 2 ,
from real and served starts, and for paths consulted ahead (socket
names), never under capability mode,
.Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH ,
.Dv RINROOT ,
.Dv O_NAMEDATTR ,
.Dv NOSYMLINKS ,
.Dv NOMAGICLINKS ,
.Dv NOXDEV ,
or for an empty path.
Each rewrite counts as one step toward
.Dv MAXSYMLINKS
.Pq Er ELOOP ,
shared with the symbolic links the lookup follows.
A restart of the same lookup applies the same answer without a new
event.
The audit record holds the rewritten path.
The result is a real vnode, with every vnode check.
.Pp
.Sy Symbolic links .
A user-origin lookup answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
(or rewritten by
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL )
is consulted again at every symbolic link it follows, with the remaining
path after the link's target was inserted,
.Dv DQ_RF_SYMLINK_RESTART ,
and start
.Dv DQ_START_REAL_ROOT
(an absolute target) or
.Dv DQ_START_REAL_SYMDIR
(a relative one; the hint is the link's directory).
It takes
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS ,
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL ,
the operation's served kinds or an errno; a signal while waiting is
.Er EINTR
under
.Dv DQ_QF_INTR
and otherwise ends nothing.
Paths consulted ahead, second paths, the kernel's own lookups, exempt
regions and capability-restricted lookups
.Pq Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH , RINROOT , No capability mode
keep their first answer.
.Pp
.Sy Audit .
A lookup from a served directory records the path as given (not made
absolute: there is no vnode to make it absolute against) and the start's
object id
.Pq Ql dq_start ;
a served descriptor answered records its id
.Pq Ql dq_object .
.Ss Served working directory and root
From stage 1c-iii-c a served directory can be an attached process's
working directory and root.
The process's real working directory and root
.Pq the kernel's Vt struct pwd
do not change: the served ones are held beside them, by reference to the
directory's file, and are inherited with the attachment.
They are set by
.Xr chdir 2
or
.Xr chroot 2
of a name the controller answers
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
of a served directory
.Pq a Dv DQ_KIND_FILE No answer is Er ENOTDIR ;
the kernel checks search permission on the presented identity
.Pq Er EACCES ,
.Xr chroot 2
also its privilege rule and
.Va kern.chroot_allow_open_directories ) ;
by
.Xr fchdir 2
or
.Xr fchroot 2
of a served directory, which for a served descriptor of another kind is
.Er ENOTDIR
first, whoever holds it and whatever its state (as
.Xr fchdir 2
of a regular file is, before any other check), then for an unattached
process (the controller included)
.Er EACCES
and for one attached to another queue
.Er EDEADLK ,
opened with
.Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH
.Er ENOTCAPABLE ,
and once revoked
.Er EIO ;
and by
.Dv DQ_IOC_ATTACH Ns 's
.Va da_rootfd
and
.Va da_cwdfd :
live served directories of the queue
.Pq Er EINVAL No for another descriptor, Er ENOTDIR No for another kind, Er EIO No once revoked ,
whose
.Vt struct file
the attachment shares with the controller's descriptor (its flags and
credential are not used for lookups; closing the controller's
descriptor keeps the start).
With a root and no
.Va da_cwdfd
the working directory is the root
.Po
.Er EIO
if the root's file reference count is exhausted: the start would be
dead, as at inheritance
.Pc ;
a
.Va da_cwdfd
must be the root or a served directory under it
.Pq Er EINVAL .
.Pp
The open-directory rule of
.Dv DQ_IOC_ATTACH
is
.Xr chroot 2 Ns 's
rule exactly, applied when a root is given:
.Va kern.chroot_allow_open_directories
0 refuses a child with any directory open, 1 only a child that is
already chrooted, 2 none
.Pq Er EPERM .
So with the default of 1 and a child that is not chrooted nothing is
refused, and the real directory descriptors the child inherited stay
usable: they start real lookups outside the served namespace, which the
controller sees as real starts and decides.
A controller that wants confinement passes
.Dv DQ_ATTACH_F_NODIRS
in
.Va da_flags ,
which, with or without a root and whatever the sysctl says, refuses a
child with any real directory open
.Pq Er EBUSY ;
.Xr libdq 3 Ns 's
.Fn dq_serve_tree
then refuses real-start lookups of names outside its tree with
.Er EACCES
unless
.Dv DQ_TREE_F_OUTSIDE_PASS
is set.
Other
.Va da_flags
bits are
.Er EINVAL .
A real
.Xr chdir 2
or
.Xr chroot 2
(one answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
or
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL ,
or a
.Xr fchdir 2
to a real directory) clears the served one.
For
.Va kern.chroot_allow_open_directories ,
the open served directories of an attached process count as open
directories, and a served root counts as being chrooted; an unattached
process's served directories start no lookups and do not count.
.Pp
Under a served root an absolute name, and under a served working
directory a relative
.Dv AT_FDCWD
name, start at the served directory
.Pq Dv DQ_START_SERVED ,
as a served directory descriptor does: never
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS ,
the operation's served kinds,
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
or an errno.
The Linux ABI root is not used.
Every
.Dv DQ_OP_RESOLVE
event of a process with a served root carries the root's object in
.Va drs_root
and its
.Va di_path
as a fifth string
.Pq Va drs_rootpathlen ,
so the controller resolves absolute names, absolute symbolic link
targets and
.Ql ..
at the root, as
.Xr chroot 2
does; it clamps
.Ql ..
at the root.
The kernel holds every served result
.Pq Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD , ATTR , LINK
under a served root, from a served start or a real one, to the root's
subtree (its
.Va di_path
at or under the root's at a component boundary), else
.Er ENOTCAPABLE
and the object retires.
A symbolic link with an absolute target met by a lookup of a process
with a served root is consulted again from the served root
.Pq Dv DQ_START_SERVED , DQ_RF_SYMLINK_RESTART ;
a second path of
.Xr rename 2
or
.Xr link 2
meeting one is
.Er EXDEV
and a path consulted ahead
.Er EIO .
The working directory and root are read with the process's
.Vt struct pwd
in one critical section, so a lookup racing a
.Xr chdir 2
never starts from a mix of the two, and the lookup keeps the served root
it started under until it ends: a symbolic link it follows re-enters at
that root even if another thread changes the root meanwhile.
A revoked or closing served directory, and one whose reference could not
be taken (at inheritance or when a lookup starts), makes lookups from it
.Er EIO .
A served root whose reference could not be taken makes every consulted
lookup of the process
.Er EIO
before any event is posted, from a served start or a real one: without
the root's path the kernel cannot hold the answer to its subtree.
.Pp
.Sy Kernel lookups .
A lookup the kernel makes for itself or for a kernel consumer
.Po
.Xr ktrace 2 ,
.Xr acct 2 ,
.Xr swapon 2 ,
.Xr swapoff 2 ,
.Xr kldload 2
of a path,
.Xr mount 2 Ns 's
path options,
.Xr quotactl 2 ,
.Xr extattrctl 2 ,
.Xr auditctl 2 ,
a jail's path, an unmarked user-space lookup
.Pc
that would start at a served working directory or root is refused
.Er EXDEV
with no event
.Pq Va ds2_resolve_exdev .
The exception is the image's interpreter (the ELF
.Dv PT_INTERP
and a
.Ql #!
line): under a served root its absolute path is consulted at the served
root
.Pq Dv DQ_ROP_EXEC_INTERP , DQ_RCLASS_KERNEL , DQ_START_SERVED ,
accepting only
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
or an errno, so the interpreter comes from the served tree; a relative
.Ql #!
interpreter under a served working directory is
.Er EXDEV .
The ELF interpreter is consulted after the old image was replaced: an
errno or a missed deadline kills the new image (it cannot fail back into
the caller), as for the kernel's other lookups during
.Xr execve 2 .
Lookups in exempt regions (the linker's search, the kernel's own paths)
use the real working directory and root.
A kernel-space path a subsystem looks up where it cannot be consulted
.Po
.Xr md 4
vnode backing files and the others the ledger lists as refused
.Pc ,
and a kernel-space lookup no intent marks outside an exempt region, are
.Er EPERM
for an attached process, with no event.
.Pp
.Sy Presented paths .
A served object's
.Va di_path
is the controller's claim.
Under a served root it is presented relative to the root's
.Va di_path ,
cut at a component boundary (the root itself is
.Ql / ) ,
and a
.Va di_path
not underneath has no presented path.
This applies to the process's own
.Xr getcwd 3 ,
.Xr realpath 3
of a name answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_ATTR ,
.Va kf_path
of its descriptors and of its working directory and root rows in
.Dv KERN_PROC_FILEDESC
and
.Dv KERN_PROC_CWD ,
and
.Xr linprocfs 4 Ns 's
.Pa cwd ,
.Pa root
and
.Pa fd/
links, including through
.Pa /proc/self .
.Xr getcwd 3
of a working directory not under the served root, and
.Xr realpath 3
of a real object (a name answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
from a real start or
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL ,
including
.Xr realpath 3
answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL )
under a served root, are
.Er ENOENT ;
a buffer too short is
.Er ERANGE .
A real object (a file opened through
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
or
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL ,
a substitute, an inherited descriptor; the reader's own or another
process's) shows no path to a reader whose root is served either: an
empty
.Va kf_path
in
.Dv F_KINFO
and
.Dv KERN_PROC_FILEDESC ,
an empty
.Va kve_path
in
.Dv KERN_PROC_VMMAP
and no name in
.Xr linprocfs 4 Ns 's
.Pa maps ,
and
.Er ENOENT
from
.Xr readlink 2
of its
.Xr fdescfs 4
link, as when
.Fn vn_fullpath
fails; the path it has is the controller's, not the program's (declared;
owner question Q1, whose other answer would present one under the root
relative to it).
Every other reader sees the vnode's path as before, and an unattached
reader pays one test of its own process for it.
With a real root,
.Va di_path
is shown as given: it must then be a real-namespace path, an obligation
of the controller.
Another process's paths are presented against the reader, as a real
path is relative to the reader's root: a reader attached to the same
queue with a served root sees them against its own root (an empty
.Va kf_path
when not underneath, as for an unreachable vnode); every other reader
(unattached, attached elsewhere, or with a real root) sees the raw
.Va di_path ,
the controller's namespace.
A revoked served working directory or root keeps its last presented path
in
.Xr getcwd 3
and in its row; one whose reference could not be taken at inheritance
has no row and
.Xr getcwd 3
is
.Er EIO ;
neither is ever replaced by the real directory.
Real objects' rows keep the real vnode's path.
.Pp
.Sy Exec .
Under a served working directory or root the image's
.Dv AT_EXECPATH
is the name in the served namespace: an absolute name as given, a
relative one joined to the served working directory's presented path
(a leading
.Ql ./
dropped).
The join is lexical: through a symbolic link to a directory it keeps the
link's name, where an unattached process gets the resolved path.
.Xr procstat 1 Fl b
and
.Va kern.proc.pathname
show the real image.
.Sh READ-ONLY OBJECTS
A descriptor minted without
.Dv DQ_MINT_F_WRITE
answers the mutating data operations as a read-only descriptor on a real
file does, and the operations no stage serves yet answer a declared value.
None posts an event.
Every row is pinned by
.Pa tests/sys/dq/dq_stage1a_test .
.Bl -tag -width "ioctl(FIOBMAP2)" -offset indent
.It Xr write 2 , Xr pwrite 2 , Xr writev 2 , Xr posix_fallocate 2 , Xr fspacectl 2
.Er EBADF :
the descriptor has no
.Dv FWRITE
and the generic layer refuses before the fileop.
.It Xr ftruncate 2
.Er EINVAL ,
the generic layer's answer for every non-writable descriptor.
.It Xr fchmod 2 , Xr fchown 2 , Xr futimens 2 , Xr fchflags 2
served as
.Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR
.Pq ownership governs, see Sx Attributes ;
the kernel's own refusals (set-id bits, a non-owner, a system flag
without privilege) come first and post nothing.
.It Xr fsync 2 , Xr fdatasync 2
0 without an event under
.Va dp_write
.Dv DQ_DENY ;
an event under
.Dv DQ_EVENT .
.Xr posix_fadvise 2
accepts every valid advice.
.It Xr fcntl 2 write locks
.Er EBADF ,
as on a read-only real file; read locks and
.Xr flock 2
are events (see
.Sx Locks ) .
.It Xr mmap 2
As a read-only real file: private and shared read mappings, a private
writable one; a shared writable one is
.Er EACCES
.Pq see Sx Pages .
.It Xr fexecve 2
.Er EACCES ,
as for a file the caller may not execute: a served file has no image to
execute before stage 3 (declared).
The generic answer for a descriptor without a vnode would be
.Er EINVAL .
.It Xr aio_read 2 , Xr aio_write 2 , Xr aio_fsync 2
refused unless
.Va vfs.aio.enable_unsafe
is set:
.Xr aio 4
calls only a local vnode safe.
With it set the aio daemon posts the event, flagged
.Dv DQ_CALLER_F_KERNEL
with the requester's credential.
.It Xr sendfile 2
served
.Pq see Sx Pages .
.It Xr copy_file_range 2
.Er EINVAL :
no vnode exists to copy between.
.It ACLs, extended attributes
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.It Xr fcntl 2 Dv F_READAHEAD , F_RDAHEAD , F_ISUNIONSTACK
0, as a regular file.
The kernel-private read/write hint commands reached through the Linux
compatibility layer answer 0 and
.Er EPERM .
.It Xr fcntl 2 Dv F_ADD_SEALS , F_GET_SEALS
.Er EINVAL .
.It Xr getdirentries 2
.Er ENOTDIR ,
the generic layer's answer for every non-directory.
.It Xr ioctl 2
see
.Sx Ioctls on a served file ;
under
.Dv DQ_DENY
every command but the local ones is
.Er ENOTTY ,
.Dv FIODTYPE
included.
.It ioctl(FIOBMAP2)
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ;
a real file runs
.Dv VOP_BMAP .
A declared difference.
.It Xr lseek 2
Local:
.Dv SEEK_SET ,
.Dv SEEK_CUR
and
.Dv SEEK_END
as a vnode
.Pq Er EINVAL , EOVERFLOW ;
.Dv SEEK_DATA
and
.Dv SEEK_HOLE
treat the file as one extent, so an offset at or beyond the size is
.Er ENXIO
and
.Dv SEEK_HOLE
answers the size.
.It Xr poll 2 , Xr select 2
Always ready for the standard events, as a regular file.
.It Xr fstatfs 2
The identity's statfs, unfiltered: no mount exists for
.Fn prison_enforce_statfs
to run, so a jailed requester sees what the controller presented.
A declared difference.
.It Xr mac_get_fd 3
.Er EINVAL :
the label system calls dispatch on the file type and have no arm for a
served file, which carries no label of its own (see
.Sx MAC ) .
.It Va f_cred
The controller's credential, see
.Sx Delivery of a minted descriptor .
.El
.Sh STAGE 1B
Stage 1b-i serves writes, attributes, sync, space, locks, ioctls and
kqueue notes; stage 1b-ii serves pages: mappings,
.Xr msync 2 ,
.Xr sendfile 2 ,
the data cache,
.Dv DQ_PUSH_INVALIDATE
and the
.Dv DQ_OP_READ_ASYNC
and
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE_ASYNC
page operations, all described above.
What they moved from stage 1a's pins: the write family on a writable
object, the attribute calls on every object, locks (no longer
.Er EBADF
and
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
from a table without lock flags), event ioctls,
.Xr fsync 2
under
.Dv DQ_EVENT ,
.Xr mmap 2
(no longer
.Er ENODEV )
and
.Xr sendfile 2
(no longer
.Er EINVAL ) .
Declared differences that remain:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
No rollback across the chunks of a large write (see
.Sx Writes ) .
.It
A mapping keeps the open file alive: the
.Xr flock 2
and OFD release,
.Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
and
.Dv NOTE_CLOSE
happen at the last
.Xr munmap 2
when a mapping outlives the descriptor, where a vnode releases at the last
close.
.It
A controller faulting on its own served mapping waits until the deadline
and gets
.Dv SIGBUS .
.It
.Xr msync 2
with
.Dv MS_SYNC
is not interruptible (its wait is bounded by the deadline) and fails with
.Er EIO
when the controller refuses a run or misses the deadline.
.It
A failed page fill aborts a
.Xr sendfile 2 ;
the socket reports
.Er EIO ,
not necessarily the call.
.It
.Dv PROT_EXEC
is governed, of the policy, by the presented
.Dv MNT_NOEXEC
alone.
.It
A mapping made into another process's map does not hold the file; its
dirty pages can be dropped at close.
.It
A revoke or the queue closing drops dirty pages the controller had not
taken.
.It
The controller's word is the end of file of an append; the kernel applies
.Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE
to an append at the presented size.
.El
.Sh STAGE 1C-I
Stage 1c-i adds attachment and consultation
.Pq Sx Attachment and resolution :
a controller sees, and may refuse, every name an attached process
resolves, before any served object can stand in for a name.
Consultations answer only
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
or an errno; served results, served directories, a virtual working
directory and root
.Pq Va da_rootfd , da_cwdfd
come in stage 1c-iii, the rules on passing served descriptors to
unattached processes in 1c-iv.
Stage 1c-ii adds
.Xr socket 2
and
.Xr socketpair 2
.Pq Dv DQ_ROP_SOCKET , DQ_ROP_SOCKETPAIR ,
answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
or an errno.
Stage 1c-iii-a adds served answers from real starts
.Pq Sx Served answers ;
stage 1c-iii-b served directories and symbolic links, served directories
as lookup starts,
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
and the re-consultation at symbolic links
.Pq Sx Served directories and starts ;
stage 1c-iii-c a served working directory and root
.Pq Sx Served working directory and root .
Consequences of the attachment that differ from an unattached run, by
design: set-id and authority transitions do not happen,
.Xr jail_attach 2 ,
.Xr setcred 2
and
.Fn __mac_set_proc
are refused, no core is dumped, and lookups skip the lockless fast path
(the results are the same; the probes of that path do not fire).
.Pa tests/sys/dq/dqpass.expected
lists the test cases these consequences change.
.Sh MAC
.Xr posix_fallocate 2
and
.Xr fspacectl 2
run
.Fn mac_file_check_write
at the boundary for every descriptor type (a vnode is then checked twice,
as
.Xr read 2
and
.Xr write 2
already are), and the Linux write and
.Dv FS_IOC_SETFLAGS
paths run the file hooks the native calls run.
A served object carries no label: under
.Xr mac_abac 4
its read, write, stat, mmap and setattr checks evaluate the requester's
label against the label of the controller's credential captured when the
queue was opened, the label a POSIX shared memory object created by the
controller would have carried.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
All under
.Va kern.dq ,
settable at boot and at run time:
.Bl -tag -width "max_inflight_per_requester" -offset indent -compact
.It Va max_io
Largest transfer in one event; a larger read is chunked in order (131072).
.It Va queue_depth
Unread events a queue holds before dropping (1024); the bound of
.Dv DQ_IOC_SET_QUEUE_DEPTH
and the depth of observers.
.It Va max_inflight_per_object
Unanswered events per object before requesters wait for a slot (64).
.It Va max_inflight_per_requester
Unanswered events per requester, keyed by nonce or pid (256).
.It Va kernel_deadline_ms
The longest a kernel-consumer or kernel-origin consultation (classes 2
and 3) waits (5000); never 0.
.It Va max_inflight_bytes
Bytes of page operations
.Pq Dv DQ_OP_READ_ASYNC , DQ_OP_WRITE_ASYNC
a queue may have pending (64 MiB); past it write-back waits.
.It Va max_objects
Live objects per queue (65536).
.It Va default_deadline_ms
The deadline a policy of
.Dv DQ_DEADLINE_DEFAULT
gets (30000); 0 means none.
.It Va queues
Read-only: open queues.
.It Va stats.resolve_failclosed
Read-only: consultations refused without the controller's answer, over
all queues since boot (each queue's own count is
.Va ds2_resolve_failclosed ,
gone with the queue).
.It Va stats.resolve_unmarked
Read-only: kernel-space lookups by attached threads outside any intent
or exempt region, over all queues since boot
.Pq Va ds2_resolve_unmarked .
.El
.Pp
Fault injection for the tests, read-write:
.Bl -tag -width "debug.dqres.drop_completion" -offset indent -compact
.It Va debug.dqres.drop_completion
The next N served answers skip their completion; the system call
returns
.Er EIO
(the containment of
.Xr namei 9 ) .
.It Va debug.dq.fail_vm_create
The next N object creations fail as if the pager object could not be
allocated
.Er ( ENOMEM ) .
.It Va debug.dq.fail_bind_enfile
The next N requesters binding a
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
answer fail as if no file could be allocated
.Er ( ENFILE ) ;
the object retires with its
.Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE .
.It Va debug.dq.delay_reply_notify
The next N replies about an object with an open file sleep one second after
the requester is released and before the
.Fn mac_file_notify_reply
hook runs, so that a test can close the file in that window.
.It Va debug.dq.fail_link_closing
The next N
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
replies find the queue closing at their link step
.Er ( ENXIO ) .
.El
.Sh TEST KERNELS
A kernel built with
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "options DQ_TEST_OPEN"
.Ed
.Pp
is a test kernel for the
.Nm
test suite.
The configuration
.Pa GENERIC-TEST
is
.Pa GENERIC
with the test-kernel options, this one among them; the test-kernel option
ledger,
.Pa sys/conf/test-options ,
lists them.
The option requires
.Cd "device dq" ;
it is not available to the
.Pa dq.ko
module, and no published configuration carries it.
.Pp
Such a kernel must never be published.
.Pa release/scripts/check-test-kernel.sh
refuses a kernel or module built with any of them where the maintained
package publication targets and the release media, virtual machine,
Firecracker and arm image recipes run it, and
.Xr build-bsd 8
refuses to plan packages or media for a configuration that carries it.
There is no override.
Not every path that can produce a kernel runs the check;
.Xr build-bsd 8
lists the ones that do, how far each has been exercised, and the ones
that do not.
The check finds a test kernel that reaches publication by accident; it
does not protect an installed system against someone who replaces its
kernel.
.Pp
A test kernel has two more variables, absent from every other kernel:
.Bl -tag -width "debug.dq.unverified_open" -offset indent -compact
.It Va debug.dq.unverified_open
Loader tunable, read-only after boot, default 0.
Set to 1 in
.Xr loader.conf 5
it enables test access; any other value reads back as 0.
.It Va debug.dq.test_opens
Read-only: opens of
.Pa /dev/dq
that passed every check while the tunable was 1.
.El
.Pp
At present the tunable changes nothing about who may open
.Pa /dev/dq :
with it set or clear, on a test kernel as on any other, an open must be
a native 64-bit process's and must pass
.Fn priv_check PRIV_DRIVER
on a node of mode 0600, exactly as described above.
The tunable only makes the kernel count such opens and announce itself
on the console.
On a kernel without the option, setting the tunable in
.Xr loader.conf 5
has no effect at all.
.Pp
A kernel built with
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "options DQ_FAULT_INJECTION"
.Ed
.Pp
is a test kernel in the same way, with the same requirements and the same
publication refusal.
It adds fault-injection variables that widen a window a test must hit;
each holds a count, and the next that many times the kernel reaches its
point it sleeps one second there, with no lock held:
.Bl -tag -width "debug.dq.delay_postop_apply" -offset indent -compact
.It Va debug.dq.delay_swap_take
a
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_SWAP
reply, after the take and before it looks for its event, so that the
requester can leave and the reply answer
.Er ECANCELED ;
.It Va debug.dq.delay_postop_apply
a requester woken with its reply, before it applies the reply's
post-operation attributes.
.El
.Pp
The older
.Va debug.dq
fault variables
.Pq Va fail_vm_create , fail_link_closing , fail_bind_enfile , delay_reply_notify
are still in every kernel; they move under the option later.
