The kernel core's half of the queue, which runs for attached processes
only, fires the
.Li dqres
provider:
.Bl -tag -width "dqres:::subst-consume" -offset indent -compact
.It dqres:::namei-served
.Fa "uint32_t op" ,
.Fa "uint32_t kind" ,
.Fa "int errno"
(a lookup from a served start returned: the
.Dv DQ_ROP_*
operation, the answer's
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_*
kind, 0 when there was none, and the errno)
.It dqres:::subst-refuse
.Fa "uint32_t op" ,
.Fa "int reason" ,
.Fa "int errno"
(a substitute refused: at the take, the reply's errno; at the
requester's completion, the requester's.
.Va reason
is 23 an offset, 24 an unrequested
.Xr flock 2
lock, 25 an OFD lock, 26 an exclusive locked creation whose lock is not
held or cannot be taken, 27 a descriptor entry with states of its own,
28 a trailing slash)
.It dqres:::subst-install
.Fa "uint32_t op" ,
.Fa "int fd"
(the requester's new descriptor)
.It dqres:::subst-consume
.Fa "struct dq_queue *" ,
.Fa "int fd"
(the supervisor's descriptor closed at the take)
.It dqres:::present
.Fa "pid_t viewer" ,
.Fa "int kind" ,
.Fa "int errno"
(a path presented to an attached viewer:
.Va kind
1 a served object's, 2 a real object's under a served root)
.El
.Pp
Every reply also runs the
.Fn mac_file_notify_reply
